1
00:00:04,300 --> 00:00:11,073
(Day 3, morning)

2
00:00:18,715 --> 00:00:22,918
(Nangja is cleaning the main hall
all by herself.)

3
00:00:26,089 --> 00:00:28,024
Good morning.

4
00:00:28,025 --> 00:00:29,491
You look like a doll!

5
00:00:29,492 --> 00:00:30,859
No, I don't!

6
00:00:31,261 --> 00:00:32,928
- Hello.
- Good morning.

7
00:00:33,196 --> 00:00:34,396
You're both up so early.

8
00:00:34,397 --> 00:00:36,831
The older I get, the earlier I wake up.

9
00:00:36,832 --> 00:00:38,400
Then I should've been up all night.

10
00:00:40,104 --> 00:00:42,071
Can I ask you something?

11
00:00:42,372 --> 00:00:43,772
How did...

12
00:00:43,773 --> 00:00:46,442
the Dark Knight and I even come up...

13
00:00:46,443 --> 00:00:47,943
in that "likability"
conversation yesterday?

14
00:00:48,078 --> 00:00:49,412
When I was sitting here,

15
00:00:49,413 --> 00:00:51,680
you were all gathered here too.

16
00:00:52,349 --> 00:00:55,085
Gray, do you remember...

17
00:00:55,086 --> 00:00:57,520
the thing I told you earlier?

18
00:00:57,521 --> 00:01:00,523
She was actually really curious about it.

19
00:01:00,757 --> 00:01:01,657
What happened?

20
00:01:01,658 --> 00:01:03,358
It's actually not a big deal.

21
00:01:03,593 --> 00:01:06,428
We got to talk yesterday afterward.

22
00:01:06,429 --> 00:01:09,665
I gave him one vote.

23
00:01:10,133 --> 00:01:11,934
- And he only got 1 vote.
- Yeah.

24
00:01:12,069 --> 00:01:15,871
When we were smoking today,
just the three of us...

25
00:01:16,139 --> 00:01:20,109
He told him that he gave him a vote too,
and I was caught off guard.

26
00:01:20,443 --> 00:01:23,012
He got 1 vote, but he has 2 people
saying they gave him a vote.

27
00:01:23,013 --> 00:01:25,046
So that's basically what happened.

28
00:01:25,182 --> 00:01:26,748
How does that work?

29
00:01:26,983 --> 00:01:28,283
The three of you...

30
00:01:28,284 --> 00:01:30,752
are telling different stories.

31
00:01:31,087 --> 00:01:32,087
Are you the spy?

32
00:01:32,756 --> 00:01:34,290
No, I'm not.

33
00:01:34,591 --> 00:01:37,426
- But he only got 1 vote.
- One of you...

34
00:01:37,427 --> 00:01:39,895
- is lying.
- I can see why you'd be suspicious.

35
00:01:40,497 --> 00:01:44,432
But I haven't lied about anything yet.

36
00:01:44,433 --> 00:01:46,468
Really? You sure?

37
00:01:46,736 --> 00:01:48,603
Promise?

38
00:01:49,306 --> 00:01:52,774
The fact that they suspected me
of being the spy...

39
00:01:52,775 --> 00:01:56,044
based on just one situation
made me a little uncomfortable.

40
00:01:56,045 --> 00:01:57,479
Right.

41
00:01:59,582 --> 00:02:02,050
- Here comes the owner.
- You were all waiting for me.

42
00:02:03,720 --> 00:02:05,821
Boiled eggs and potatoes.

43
00:02:05,822 --> 00:02:07,523
Twelve ramyeon noodles.

44
00:02:07,524 --> 00:02:10,159
(Eggs, potatoes - 200 dollars, ramyeon
noodles - 300 dollars, Total 500 dollars)

45
00:02:10,326 --> 00:02:12,360
(500 dollars deducted
from the group fund.)

46
00:02:12,695 --> 00:02:14,596
Ramyeon for breakfast? I'm so excited.

47
00:02:14,765 --> 00:02:17,232
- I love ramyeon.
- I love it so much.

48
00:02:17,967 --> 00:02:18,934
Let's open it.

49
00:02:18,935 --> 00:02:20,769
(Cooking: Go Ae Shin)

50
00:02:20,770 --> 00:02:22,504
- You can eat it.
- Do you want me to shake it?

51
00:02:23,239 --> 00:02:25,240
- That's so thoughtful.
- Yeah.

52
00:02:26,142 --> 00:02:27,309
That comes with experience.

53
00:02:29,012 --> 00:02:30,546
I should be shredding these.

54
00:02:32,682 --> 00:02:33,882
We should help too.

55
00:02:33,883 --> 00:02:35,884
- A new announcement?
- I love your outfit.

56
00:02:36,787 --> 00:02:39,722
It is time to select today's leader.

57
00:02:41,025 --> 00:02:43,592
Based on last night's vote,

58
00:02:44,027 --> 00:02:47,162
three leader candidates were selected.

59
00:02:47,697 --> 00:02:49,197
The candidates for today's leader are,

60
00:02:49,866 --> 00:02:52,601
Michael.

61
00:02:53,069 --> 00:02:55,103
Hippo.

62
00:02:55,339 --> 00:02:58,574
And Benjamin.

63
00:02:59,676 --> 00:03:02,644
We will now hold the leader vote...

64
00:03:02,645 --> 00:03:04,847
at the Community Center.

65
00:03:06,583 --> 00:03:08,851
I received 1 vote on the first day.

66
00:03:08,852 --> 00:03:11,453
But I was really glad this morning...

67
00:03:11,621 --> 00:03:12,955
because I received 7 votes.

68
00:03:13,157 --> 00:03:14,890
I'd like to ask all of you here...

69
00:03:14,891 --> 00:03:18,427
to protect me a little.

70
00:03:18,695 --> 00:03:20,663
Sorry, Gray,

71
00:03:20,664 --> 00:03:22,598
but I've been using your name quite a bit.

72
00:03:23,333 --> 00:03:24,933
I really wanted to become the leader.

73
00:03:24,934 --> 00:03:27,936
This was when we'd finally get to see
where everyone stood.

74
00:03:27,937 --> 00:03:30,505
I thought having immunity...

75
00:03:30,506 --> 00:03:32,374
would be really important.

76
00:03:32,510 --> 00:03:35,110
I figured I'd become the leader
and get immunity for myself.

77
00:03:35,478 --> 00:03:37,145
Four people chose me.

78
00:03:37,146 --> 00:03:38,213
The previous day I received 1.

79
00:03:38,214 --> 00:03:39,281
I definitely won some people over.

80
00:03:39,649 --> 00:03:40,916
If someone were to kill me,

81
00:03:40,917 --> 00:03:42,985
I'd be out in 2 hits.

82
00:03:42,986 --> 00:03:44,954
So with only 4 points,
I knew I had to win this.

83
00:03:44,955 --> 00:03:46,588
I knew I absolutely had to win.

84
00:03:46,589 --> 00:03:48,824
Even before I got 4 votes,

85
00:03:48,825 --> 00:03:49,858
I was thinking about how to make my move.

86
00:03:49,859 --> 00:03:52,394
I thought to myself,
how can I fool them all...

87
00:03:52,395 --> 00:03:53,661
and take the throne?

88
00:03:53,662 --> 00:03:56,264
I wanted to come across as someone...

89
00:03:56,499 --> 00:03:58,166
who works really hard.

90
00:03:58,167 --> 00:03:59,801
So I'd sit there with a book.

91
00:03:59,802 --> 00:04:01,837
Opening and closing it for show.

92
00:04:01,971 --> 00:04:04,067
(Day 3 Leader candidates)

93
00:04:04,068 --> 00:04:06,241
(Benjamin, Hippo, Michael)

94
00:04:06,876 --> 00:04:09,611
(Day 2 Leader's Term Ends)

95
00:04:10,313 --> 00:04:13,249
I guess people wanted
a different kind of leader.

96
00:04:13,250 --> 00:04:16,652
At first, the outgoing people
were the ones getting the votes.

97
00:04:16,653 --> 00:04:18,888
Maybe they're looking for someone...

98
00:04:18,889 --> 00:04:21,990
who quietly brings the Community together.

99
00:04:23,762 --> 00:04:25,995
All right, let's have our three
candidates come forward.

100
00:04:26,462 --> 00:04:27,395
Okay.

101
00:04:27,730 --> 00:04:30,732
(The Three Candidates' Campaign Speeches)

102
00:04:30,733 --> 00:04:32,300
- A bit nervous?
- Yes.

103
00:04:32,870 --> 00:04:35,037
We'll now hear the campaign speeches...

104
00:04:35,038 --> 00:04:37,039
for the Day 3 leader election.

105
00:04:38,274 --> 00:04:40,208
Here are our candidates.
Let's give them a round of applause.

106
00:04:41,611 --> 00:04:43,679
It's getting more elaborate.

107
00:04:44,280 --> 00:04:47,249
First up, let's hear
from Candidate No. 1, Michael.

108
00:04:47,250 --> 00:04:48,483
He's a natural at this.

109
00:04:49,285 --> 00:04:52,454
First, as you can see from my outfit,

110
00:04:52,455 --> 00:04:53,889
I'm ready to work today.

111
00:04:55,224 --> 00:04:57,792
As for my second practical promise,

112
00:04:57,793 --> 00:05:00,762
I've always been a strategist
on other survival shows.

113
00:05:00,763 --> 00:05:03,464
My specialty is coming up with strategies
and assigning roles.

114
00:05:03,465 --> 00:05:05,033
Adapting to situations.

115
00:05:05,034 --> 00:05:06,234
I'm good at navigating social situations.

116
00:05:06,502 --> 00:05:08,336
And lastly, as a bonus...

117
00:05:08,337 --> 00:05:10,305
when everything's over later,

118
00:05:11,474 --> 00:05:12,507
I'll do a rap for you.

119
00:05:13,943 --> 00:05:15,444
Wow, that's a big one.

120
00:05:15,879 --> 00:05:18,013
- I'm desperate.
- Okay.

121
00:05:18,114 --> 00:05:21,249
Oh, no. I feel like I have to do
something like that too.

122
00:05:21,250 --> 00:05:22,851
He said he has hands-on experience...

123
00:05:22,852 --> 00:05:26,188
and that he'll even keep us
entertained at the end.

124
00:05:26,956 --> 00:05:28,857
Let's hear what Hippo has to offer.

125
00:05:28,858 --> 00:05:30,558
(Candidate No. 2 Hippo)

126
00:05:30,559 --> 00:05:31,726
First of all,

127
00:05:31,995 --> 00:05:35,197
thank you so much
for giving me your votes.

128
00:05:35,431 --> 00:05:39,869
For me, it's really important that we feel
safe communicating with each other.

129
00:05:39,870 --> 00:05:42,637
And that we trust one another.

130
00:05:42,638 --> 00:05:45,841
So I want to be
as transparent as possible...

131
00:05:45,842 --> 00:05:48,610
and assign roles that allow...

132
00:05:48,611 --> 00:05:51,146
everyone to fully show who they are.

133
00:05:51,282 --> 00:05:53,115
If I may highlight my leadership a little,

134
00:05:53,617 --> 00:05:56,118
I actually run a community myself.

135
00:05:56,219 --> 00:05:58,387
Around 300 people
have been part of it so far,

136
00:05:58,388 --> 00:06:01,490
and about 100 are currently involved.

137
00:06:01,491 --> 00:06:03,692
So I do have experience
running a community.

138
00:06:03,693 --> 00:06:07,229
I have more leadership experience
than you might think.

139
00:06:07,530 --> 00:06:08,530
Thank you.

140
00:06:09,832 --> 00:06:12,100
Michael, why do you look so stiff?

141
00:06:12,101 --> 00:06:13,234
Huh?

142
00:06:13,803 --> 00:06:16,304
Let's hear what Benjamin has to offer.

143
00:06:17,106 --> 00:06:18,640
I'm so nervous.

144
00:06:19,042 --> 00:06:20,676
Hi, everyone. I'm Benjamin.

145
00:06:21,312 --> 00:06:24,479
Everyone's so good with words.

146
00:06:25,682 --> 00:06:26,782
Well,

147
00:06:27,217 --> 00:06:31,152
I'm actually pretty quiet and introverted.

148
00:06:31,688 --> 00:06:33,689
So even talking
about what I've got to offer...

149
00:06:34,390 --> 00:06:37,559
makes me super nervous.

150
00:06:39,996 --> 00:06:42,931
I quit my job last year,

151
00:06:42,932 --> 00:06:45,834
and I was going through a lot personally.

152
00:06:46,469 --> 00:06:49,304
But yesterday really
made me realize a lot.

153
00:06:49,739 --> 00:06:51,873
We worked on a problem together.

154
00:06:52,575 --> 00:06:54,976
And I felt really sorry when I arrived.

155
00:06:55,344 --> 00:07:00,148
But seeing how warmly you all
accepted me...

156
00:07:00,683 --> 00:07:02,584
really moved me a lot.

157
00:07:02,585 --> 00:07:07,656
So I started seriously thinking about...

158
00:07:08,024 --> 00:07:11,727
what I could do for this Community.

159
00:07:13,196 --> 00:07:16,397
There's a reason
I worked up the courage...

160
00:07:16,398 --> 00:07:18,633
to do this.

161
00:07:18,634 --> 00:07:20,268
At first,

162
00:07:20,269 --> 00:07:22,637
I asked if one person takes all the money.

163
00:07:22,839 --> 00:07:24,540
But they said that wasn't the case.

164
00:07:24,541 --> 00:07:28,977
They told me many of us could
make it to the end.

165
00:07:29,612 --> 00:07:32,414
That's the kind of thing I like.

166
00:07:32,582 --> 00:07:35,117
I hope as many of us as possible
can make it to the end.

167
00:07:35,586 --> 00:07:38,654
Staying as lively and warm as we are now.

168
00:07:39,154 --> 00:07:43,258
And hopefully, we can all walk away
with a lot of money together.

169
00:07:43,259 --> 00:07:46,294
My main priority is making sure...

170
00:07:46,696 --> 00:07:48,497
as many people as possible can stay.

171
00:07:48,498 --> 00:07:50,932
And I'll do everything I can...

172
00:07:51,234 --> 00:07:52,568
to make that happen.

173
00:07:56,673 --> 00:07:58,373
The quality keeps getting better.

174
00:07:59,842 --> 00:08:02,510
I hadn't talked to Benjamin much.

175
00:08:02,511 --> 00:08:05,580
So I didn't consider voting for him
as our leader.

176
00:08:05,581 --> 00:08:09,384
But his sincere speech moved me.
So I voted for him.

177
00:08:09,385 --> 00:08:11,553
He's very quiet, but I think he'll go...

178
00:08:11,554 --> 00:08:13,521
above and beyond in everything he does.

179
00:08:13,522 --> 00:08:14,956
I think he'd make a great leader.

180
00:08:15,157 --> 00:08:17,426
Before we went to vote, Benjamin told me,

181
00:08:17,427 --> 00:08:19,428
"If I become leader, I'll protect you."

182
00:08:19,429 --> 00:08:21,597
I'll protect you.

183
00:08:21,598 --> 00:08:23,131
Oh, thank you.

184
00:08:23,132 --> 00:08:26,568
I think that gave me a bit of a boost.

185
00:08:26,569 --> 00:08:29,738
Benjamin told me he'd protect me
if I got eliminated.

186
00:08:29,739 --> 00:08:31,573
Please keep your word, Benjamin.

187
00:08:34,310 --> 00:08:35,344
It's about to be announced.

188
00:08:35,345 --> 00:08:37,045
Voting is now closed.

189
00:08:38,080 --> 00:08:40,581
The new leader
of The Community House is...

190
00:08:41,450 --> 00:08:44,619
Benjamin.

191
00:08:44,620 --> 00:08:46,421
(Day 3 Leader, Benjamin)

192
00:08:48,324 --> 00:08:49,758
- Congratulations.
- Hey, Benjamin.

193
00:08:49,759 --> 00:08:50,592
Benjamin!

194
00:08:51,093 --> 00:08:52,561
Congratulations!

195
00:08:52,562 --> 00:08:53,727
Congratulations.

196
00:08:53,728 --> 00:08:55,763
Go collect the leader box!

197
00:08:55,764 --> 00:08:56,698
I will.

198
00:08:56,699 --> 00:08:59,568
- He was so genuine today.
- Can he go get the leader box right now?

199
00:09:00,602 --> 00:09:01,803
I'll be back.

200
00:09:08,945 --> 00:09:09,978
It's a total scam.

201
00:09:11,380 --> 00:09:14,349
A dirty campaign promise,
fitting for a spy.

202
00:09:14,350 --> 00:09:16,484
So this is how democracy fails.

203
00:09:16,485 --> 00:09:19,187
From what I saw the day before,

204
00:09:19,188 --> 00:09:20,522
they all seemed like good people.

205
00:09:20,523 --> 00:09:21,956
Pretty well-established.

206
00:09:21,957 --> 00:09:24,660
Whether it's financial stability
or general knowledge,

207
00:09:24,661 --> 00:09:26,127
they're all pretty well-established.

208
00:09:26,128 --> 00:09:28,129
They're people who can't afford...

209
00:09:28,130 --> 00:09:29,864
to look like they've lost
their basic values.

210
00:09:29,865 --> 00:09:32,100
Whether it's all an act or it's genuine,

211
00:09:32,101 --> 00:09:35,069
they have certain rules
they hold themselves to.

212
00:09:35,070 --> 00:09:37,705
All I have to do is play
on those standards, right?

213
00:09:37,940 --> 00:09:39,507
Benjamin said things had been tough.

214
00:09:39,508 --> 00:09:41,343
He said he left his job last year
and had been struggling.

215
00:09:41,344 --> 00:09:42,877
And he was trembling, saying,

216
00:09:42,878 --> 00:09:46,447
"I really want to do a good job."
With all his heart.

217
00:09:46,448 --> 00:09:50,251
That's a little different from making
playful promises about rapping.

218
00:09:50,252 --> 00:09:53,689
Or confidently talking
about what you can do.

219
00:09:53,690 --> 00:09:57,059
When you look at real-life scammers,

220
00:09:57,060 --> 00:09:59,962
they put their heart and soul
into their lies.

221
00:09:59,963 --> 00:10:01,429
It's their job, after all.

222
00:10:01,430 --> 00:10:02,831
Of course they do it professionally.

223
00:10:02,832 --> 00:10:05,266
Benjamin was pretty much the same.

224
00:10:05,267 --> 00:10:07,335
Now you guys can't take me out.

225
00:10:07,904 --> 00:10:08,904
What a shame.

226
00:10:10,172 --> 00:10:12,740
Congratulations on becoming leader.

227
00:10:12,741 --> 00:10:14,909
Please collect the leader box.

228
00:10:15,645 --> 00:10:17,646
You've all really caused
some serious chaos.

229
00:10:24,520 --> 00:10:25,620
(Tensions Under the Roof)

230
00:10:25,855 --> 00:10:28,123
(The Community)

231
00:10:28,691 --> 00:10:30,091
A golden badge.

232
00:10:30,359 --> 00:10:31,592
Yeah, a golden badge.

233
00:10:31,593 --> 00:10:32,627
(4. Your Community)

234
00:10:43,439 --> 00:10:45,740
(Reporter Selection)

235
00:10:45,741 --> 00:10:46,775
A reporter?

236
00:10:48,411 --> 00:10:49,945
- A reporter selection?
- A reporter?

237
00:10:51,448 --> 00:10:53,315
Starting today,

238
00:10:53,816 --> 00:10:56,751
the residents may select
a Reporter by unanimous agreement.

239
00:10:57,186 --> 00:10:58,787
The Reporter selected today...

240
00:10:59,088 --> 00:11:02,123
will remain in the role
until the end of your stay.

241
00:11:02,858 --> 00:11:04,125
Once a day,

242
00:11:04,593 --> 00:11:08,163
the Reporter may check
the Community Center...

243
00:11:08,597 --> 00:11:11,599
for information they believe
all residents need to know.

244
00:11:12,201 --> 00:11:17,372
However, the Reporter does not have
to disclose the information as received.

245
00:11:17,373 --> 00:11:18,340
The role of the media.

246
00:11:18,975 --> 00:11:20,575
That's a powerful role.

247
00:11:21,244 --> 00:11:23,778
Please discuss among yourselves...

248
00:11:23,779 --> 00:11:25,981
and select a Reporter within 20 minutes.

249
00:11:26,615 --> 00:11:30,251
The selected Reporter should then
go to the Community Center...

250
00:11:30,252 --> 00:11:32,220
to collect their press ID.

251
00:11:32,455 --> 00:11:35,223
Wow, that's a really powerful role.

252
00:11:36,025 --> 00:11:38,326
The person with access
to the best information.

253
00:11:38,327 --> 00:11:40,028
It'll probably give us a crucial clue.

254
00:11:40,029 --> 00:11:41,396
To uncover what's been hidden.

255
00:11:41,397 --> 00:11:43,599
What if the spy becomes the Reporter?

256
00:11:43,600 --> 00:11:45,767
If that happens...

257
00:11:45,768 --> 00:11:47,268
they'd have all that information
to themselves.

258
00:11:47,269 --> 00:11:48,536
Oh, my god.

259
00:11:49,071 --> 00:11:52,074
It could give them a huge amount of power.

260
00:11:52,075 --> 00:11:53,341
Or they could become the spy's target.

261
00:11:53,342 --> 00:11:55,743
Worst case, the spy becomes the Reporter.

262
00:11:58,647 --> 00:12:00,381
Wow, so this is a bit of a gamble.

263
00:12:00,683 --> 00:12:02,683
The Reporter could play a major role...

264
00:12:02,684 --> 00:12:06,054
in keeping the spy in check.

265
00:12:06,055 --> 00:12:07,390
I was pretty glad about it.

266
00:12:07,391 --> 00:12:08,890
It could actually work in our favor.

267
00:12:08,991 --> 00:12:10,625
If my identity gets exposed,

268
00:12:10,626 --> 00:12:14,329
the Reporter can just say,
"Benjamin's the spy." Then it's game over.

269
00:12:14,497 --> 00:12:17,232
Having that possibility
hanging over me isn't great.

270
00:12:17,833 --> 00:12:20,001
But can the Reporter become the leader?

271
00:12:20,002 --> 00:12:21,903
The Reporter has a lot of power.

272
00:12:22,471 --> 00:12:24,172
And so does the leader.

273
00:12:24,507 --> 00:12:27,175
Combine the two, and you've got
the media colluding with the one in power.

274
00:12:28,277 --> 00:12:29,877
- You're right.
- So...

275
00:12:30,113 --> 00:12:33,815
I think it'd be a good idea to make a rule
that the Reporter can't be the leader.

276
00:12:33,816 --> 00:12:35,717
To keep the two roles separate.

277
00:12:35,718 --> 00:12:37,619
We really don't know what could happen.

278
00:12:37,620 --> 00:12:38,487
Exactly.

279
00:12:38,488 --> 00:12:39,488
I agree.

280
00:12:39,489 --> 00:12:40,789
I agree too.

281
00:12:40,790 --> 00:12:41,890
- Agreed.
- I agree.

282
00:12:42,124 --> 00:12:43,692
If you have the power
to access information,

283
00:12:43,693 --> 00:12:45,892
without checks, you'll inevitably
become corrupt.

284
00:12:45,893 --> 00:12:49,030
Or you could end up serving
a particular faction.

285
00:12:49,031 --> 00:12:51,399
So my first priority was to make sure...

286
00:12:51,400 --> 00:12:53,067
too much power wasn't concentrated.

287
00:12:53,303 --> 00:12:57,405
The Reporter is a medium
that can sway public opinion.

288
00:12:57,406 --> 00:12:59,240
That's what you call journalism.

289
00:12:59,241 --> 00:13:00,875
How people view a government.

290
00:13:00,876 --> 00:13:03,111
How people view a company or product.

291
00:13:03,112 --> 00:13:05,447
How people view
a public figure or celebrity.

292
00:13:05,648 --> 00:13:07,649
That's an enormous amount of power.

293
00:13:07,950 --> 00:13:09,683
And I think there's a reason...

294
00:13:09,684 --> 00:13:11,953
they didn't make it
mandatory for the Reporter...

295
00:13:11,954 --> 00:13:13,688
- to disclose the information.
- True.

296
00:13:13,689 --> 00:13:16,224
They might learn something...

297
00:13:16,225 --> 00:13:17,392
that could divide the Community.

298
00:13:17,393 --> 00:13:19,094
Whether they reveal it or not...

299
00:13:19,095 --> 00:13:21,329
depends on what they want...

300
00:13:21,330 --> 00:13:22,964
for the Community and how they think...

301
00:13:22,965 --> 00:13:24,966
it should move forward.

302
00:13:24,967 --> 00:13:27,468
- Keep it under embargo for a bit.
- Exactly.

303
00:13:27,469 --> 00:13:29,237
You can keep it under embargo,

304
00:13:29,371 --> 00:13:31,806
but you don't distort it
when you report it.

305
00:13:32,241 --> 00:13:33,908
It should be someone we can trust.

306
00:13:33,909 --> 00:13:36,178
Even if they tell a white lie.

307
00:13:36,179 --> 00:13:37,946
Basically, someone who loves peace.

308
00:13:37,947 --> 00:13:39,113
And is honest.

309
00:13:39,381 --> 00:13:41,382
I have a nomination.
I'd like to nominate Nangja.

310
00:13:41,751 --> 00:13:43,618
I'd like to nominate Nangja too.

311
00:13:43,619 --> 00:13:44,886
(Dark Knight and Hippo nominate Nangja.)

312
00:13:44,987 --> 00:13:47,555
I didn't want them to see me as a threat.

313
00:13:47,556 --> 00:13:50,725
Or exceptionally good at anything.

314
00:13:50,726 --> 00:13:52,260
If that's how they saw me,
mission accomplished.

315
00:13:52,461 --> 00:13:54,095
I understand.

316
00:13:54,897 --> 00:13:56,898
But we didn't get to figure out
her occupation.

317
00:13:57,333 --> 00:13:58,433
Because of that,

318
00:13:59,235 --> 00:14:00,502
I nominate Gray.

319
00:14:00,870 --> 00:14:01,770
Gray.

320
00:14:01,937 --> 00:14:03,738
Can I make a case for myself then?

321
00:14:03,739 --> 00:14:04,906
Of course.

322
00:14:06,008 --> 00:14:09,210
I think everyone here
has different talents.

323
00:14:09,211 --> 00:14:11,980
Not just the people
who stood out early on.

324
00:14:12,181 --> 00:14:14,249
If everything kept revolving around them,

325
00:14:14,250 --> 00:14:17,252
we'd end up with power blocs, factions,

326
00:14:17,253 --> 00:14:20,822
and eventually elitism.
So I gave it some thought.

327
00:14:20,823 --> 00:14:22,824
I'm basically a pacifist.

328
00:14:23,259 --> 00:14:25,393
I was suspected of being the spy, though.

329
00:14:25,394 --> 00:14:27,929
But I swear,

330
00:14:27,930 --> 00:14:30,031
I haven't told a single lie
since I got here.

331
00:14:30,633 --> 00:14:32,334
Were you suspected before?

332
00:14:32,335 --> 00:14:35,836
Well, I think it was because...

333
00:14:35,837 --> 00:14:37,606
I'm always so poker-faced and low-key.

334
00:14:37,607 --> 00:14:40,609
That's what some seemed to think.

335
00:14:40,910 --> 00:14:43,378
I'd like to nominate our leader
from yesterday, Polar Bear.

336
00:14:43,379 --> 00:14:44,446
Thank you.

337
00:14:45,080 --> 00:14:47,249
I wasn't nominated, but...

338
00:14:47,250 --> 00:14:49,385
since I already have immunity,

339
00:14:49,386 --> 00:14:51,486
I do have some peace of mind.

340
00:14:51,487 --> 00:14:54,823
So I figured I could be completely honest.

341
00:14:55,391 --> 00:14:56,925
To have immunity is a plus.

342
00:14:56,926 --> 00:14:58,293
That's right.

343
00:14:58,561 --> 00:14:59,962
Then let's get the voting started.

344
00:14:59,963 --> 00:15:01,696
Then we'll vote by a show of hands
between the final two.

345
00:15:01,697 --> 00:15:02,565
Okay.

346
00:15:02,566 --> 00:15:05,100
Can we do this in 50 seconds?

347
00:15:05,101 --> 00:15:06,235
Hurry up! Go!

348
00:15:06,236 --> 00:15:07,536
I'll open it right away.

349
00:15:08,037 --> 00:15:08,870
Hurry.

350
00:15:08,871 --> 00:15:09,738
Nangja.

351
00:15:10,072 --> 00:15:11,406
Nangja. Gray.

352
00:15:11,407 --> 00:15:12,440
Gray. Nangja.

353
00:15:12,441 --> 00:15:14,075
Genie. Polar Bear. Gray.

354
00:15:14,777 --> 00:15:16,846
It's between Nangja and Gray.

355
00:15:16,847 --> 00:15:18,012
We need to choose right now.

356
00:15:18,013 --> 00:15:19,514
We'll vote by a show of hands
between the final two.

357
00:15:19,515 --> 00:15:22,150
Raise your hand if you want
Nangja to be the Reporter.

358
00:15:22,151 --> 00:15:23,585
I think I can do it.

359
00:15:23,586 --> 00:15:26,287
One, two, three, four, five,
and you have your hand up?

360
00:15:26,288 --> 00:15:27,789
(Day 2 Inner Team 6 people, Nangja)

361
00:15:27,923 --> 00:15:28,856
And Gray?

362
00:15:29,024 --> 00:15:30,257
- Exactly six?
- It's a tie.

363
00:15:31,060 --> 00:15:33,028
(Day 2 Income Team 6 people, Gray)

364
00:15:33,029 --> 00:15:34,362
We end up in ties a lot.

365
00:15:34,363 --> 00:15:35,230
Indeed.

366
00:15:35,231 --> 00:15:37,498
It's half and half. 6 to 6.

367
00:15:37,499 --> 00:15:40,435
Since it's a tie, I think the leader
should make the final call.

368
00:15:40,436 --> 00:15:42,437
That's the original result, right?

369
00:15:42,438 --> 00:15:44,772
Then I think we should respect that.

370
00:15:44,773 --> 00:15:45,640
Alright.

371
00:15:45,641 --> 00:15:48,243
(Nangja is elected as the Reporter.)

372
00:15:48,244 --> 00:15:52,480
We're counting on you, Reporter.

373
00:15:56,819 --> 00:15:59,353
This whole concept of a Reporter
makes it so fun.

374
00:16:08,230 --> 00:16:09,263
"Press."

375
00:16:10,400 --> 00:16:12,400
The Reporter can access information...

376
00:16:12,401 --> 00:16:15,069
from the end of the evening activities,

377
00:16:16,038 --> 00:16:17,305
until midnight.

378
00:16:17,739 --> 00:16:20,508
So I could come here
without anyone knowing.

379
00:16:21,878 --> 00:16:24,345
I could make sure no one knows I'm coming.

380
00:16:25,414 --> 00:16:29,517
The Reporter role comes
with a tremendous amount of power.

381
00:16:29,518 --> 00:16:31,986
But what reason
do they have to trust me...

382
00:16:31,987 --> 00:16:33,855
and believe everything I show them?

383
00:16:34,257 --> 00:16:36,524
Bingo! Let's enjoy this.

384
00:16:40,162 --> 00:16:41,596
You made me the Reporter, huh.

385
00:16:43,832 --> 00:16:46,934
Do you really trust me that much?

386
00:16:53,942 --> 00:16:55,142
It was a close vote.

387
00:16:56,278 --> 00:16:59,847
I honestly thought you'd win in the end.

388
00:16:59,848 --> 00:17:02,083
- But while we were gone yesterday.
- Right.

389
00:17:02,084 --> 00:17:04,218
People formed a faction.

390
00:17:04,853 --> 00:17:07,688
I think they formed a group...

391
00:17:07,689 --> 00:17:10,191
while we were out yesterday.

392
00:17:10,192 --> 00:17:13,360
Exactly. I think that's why they tried
to paint me as the spy.

393
00:17:14,663 --> 00:17:15,663
Are you the spy?

394
00:17:16,465 --> 00:17:18,032
No, I'm not.

395
00:17:18,300 --> 00:17:20,268
But I haven't lied about anything yet.

396
00:17:20,436 --> 00:17:22,504
But that group is actually suspecting...

397
00:17:22,505 --> 00:17:24,239
Michael of being the spy.

398
00:17:25,007 --> 00:17:26,841
Even so, it brought the rest of them...

399
00:17:26,842 --> 00:17:28,342
closer together.

400
00:17:29,145 --> 00:17:30,445
With Nangja at the center?

401
00:17:33,916 --> 00:17:35,617
We need to start coming up with a plan.

402
00:17:35,618 --> 00:17:37,185
Yeah, we should.

403
00:17:39,788 --> 00:17:40,688
The royal inspector's badge!

404
00:17:41,323 --> 00:17:42,224
The royal inspector's badge.

405
00:17:42,225 --> 00:17:43,991
- There's a press ID.
- It's a royal inspector's badge.

406
00:17:44,560 --> 00:17:45,659
It's a press ID.

407
00:17:46,795 --> 00:17:48,863
I'll show it to the leader first.

408
00:17:48,864 --> 00:17:49,864
Oh, okay.

409
00:17:51,167 --> 00:17:52,033
Right?

410
00:17:52,534 --> 00:17:53,868
Yeah, Michael too.

411
00:17:56,572 --> 00:17:58,973
So the information the Community
gives the Reporter,

412
00:17:58,974 --> 00:18:00,942
is it in response to something they ask?

413
00:18:00,943 --> 00:18:02,977
Or do they just give
the Reporter information?

414
00:18:02,978 --> 00:18:05,180
They give you the information you want.

415
00:18:05,481 --> 00:18:09,584
However, there are some things
they can't tell you.

416
00:18:09,585 --> 00:18:11,219
Fair enough.

417
00:18:11,520 --> 00:18:13,788
Then the questions would really matter.

418
00:18:16,859 --> 00:18:19,761
I think we need to set
some ground rules...

419
00:18:19,762 --> 00:18:21,896
among ourselves
about the Reporter's role and authority.

420
00:18:22,631 --> 00:18:24,632
I think we could also limit
the Reporter...

421
00:18:24,633 --> 00:18:28,365
to asking questions
that benefit the Community.

422
00:18:28,366 --> 00:18:29,487
For example,

423
00:18:29,838 --> 00:18:32,607
"What is someone's political view?"

424
00:18:32,608 --> 00:18:35,777
That kind of information
could be used to target someone directly.

425
00:18:36,078 --> 00:18:38,813
Instead of asking
for personal information like that,

426
00:18:39,047 --> 00:18:40,948
we could ask something like,

427
00:18:40,949 --> 00:18:43,517
"How many people will be taking part
in tomorrow's mission?"

428
00:18:43,518 --> 00:18:46,353
I think the Reporter's role...

429
00:18:46,354 --> 00:18:47,989
is to get information...

430
00:18:47,990 --> 00:18:50,057
that's as useful
to the Community as possible.

431
00:18:50,727 --> 00:18:52,860
The way I see it,

432
00:18:52,861 --> 00:18:54,562
having information is key.

433
00:18:54,563 --> 00:18:56,263
We get new rules every day.

434
00:18:56,264 --> 00:18:57,832
And there are things
that we don't know yet.

435
00:18:58,266 --> 00:19:00,168
Like what the spy's role is.

436
00:19:00,169 --> 00:19:02,537
What's in it for the spy?

437
00:19:02,538 --> 00:19:03,971
Why are they here?

438
00:19:04,173 --> 00:19:06,307
They must have a purpose
and a mission, right?

439
00:19:07,043 --> 00:19:10,645
I think it's important
to figure that out first.

440
00:19:11,246 --> 00:19:13,714
So we're basically split into two sides.

441
00:19:13,715 --> 00:19:17,285
Whether we focus on rooting out the spy.

442
00:19:17,286 --> 00:19:20,554
Or use it to help us win team games
and earn more money.

443
00:19:20,555 --> 00:19:22,056
I think it comes down to those two.

444
00:19:22,490 --> 00:19:24,325
I can speak to this...

445
00:19:24,326 --> 00:19:26,727
because something happened...

446
00:19:26,728 --> 00:19:28,730
that made me...

447
00:19:28,731 --> 00:19:31,465
really uneasy.

448
00:19:31,900 --> 00:19:35,236
As it happens, one of two people...

449
00:19:35,670 --> 00:19:37,572
has to be lying.

450
00:19:37,639 --> 00:19:39,473
It's 100 percent one or the other.

451
00:19:39,875 --> 00:19:41,910
They're both telling me
the exact same thing.

452
00:19:41,911 --> 00:19:43,578
And I still haven't figured out
who's lying.

453
00:19:44,146 --> 00:19:47,381
So, are you saying two people lied to you?

454
00:19:47,382 --> 00:19:48,917
- Both?
- No.

455
00:19:48,918 --> 00:19:51,185
- Oh, so one of the two lied.
- Yes.

456
00:19:51,186 --> 00:19:53,654
Whether it's an intentional lie...

457
00:19:53,655 --> 00:19:55,123
or an unintentional one.

458
00:19:55,724 --> 00:19:59,193
Today he brought it up...

459
00:19:59,194 --> 00:20:00,594
in front of everyone.

460
00:20:00,829 --> 00:20:04,732
I'd already gone over it
five or six times by then.

461
00:20:04,733 --> 00:20:05,866
But he brought it up again.

462
00:20:06,002 --> 00:20:09,337
That's when I got really mad,
so I jumped right in.

463
00:20:10,239 --> 00:20:12,907
I feel like this is only making
everyone more uneasy,

464
00:20:12,908 --> 00:20:14,609
so I'll just be honest.

465
00:20:14,610 --> 00:20:19,582
On the first night,
Michael and I were drinking.

466
00:20:19,583 --> 00:20:20,850
We got to talking about...

467
00:20:20,851 --> 00:20:22,450
who we'd given our favorability votes to.

468
00:20:22,451 --> 00:20:23,484
So I told him,

469
00:20:23,485 --> 00:20:26,821
"I voted for you, Michael."

470
00:20:26,822 --> 00:20:29,825
Then the next day,
the three of us were smoking.

471
00:20:30,125 --> 00:20:33,160
- And Dark Knight...
- Michael said he'd gotten one vote.

472
00:20:33,161 --> 00:20:35,362
So I told him I gave my vote to him.

473
00:20:36,198 --> 00:20:38,599
So two people claimed
they'd voted for him.

474
00:20:38,600 --> 00:20:40,735
- But he'd only gotten one vote.
- Oh, okay.

475
00:20:40,736 --> 00:20:43,004
So he's saying one person lied.

476
00:20:43,605 --> 00:20:47,975
Let me be honest.

477
00:20:48,410 --> 00:20:50,578
Michael came to me yesterday and said,

478
00:20:51,280 --> 00:20:52,680
"I suspect Gray."

479
00:20:52,681 --> 00:20:54,649
Because to Michael,

480
00:20:54,918 --> 00:20:56,584
I came off as honest.

481
00:20:56,585 --> 00:20:57,919
(Sighing)

482
00:20:57,920 --> 00:21:00,121
Okay.

483
00:21:00,122 --> 00:21:02,156
Of course I suspected
both of you at first.

484
00:21:03,959 --> 00:21:05,560
Honestly, I'm not sure this is enough...

485
00:21:05,561 --> 00:21:08,196
to cause this much division.

486
00:21:08,197 --> 00:21:10,264
It's up to all of you, of course.

487
00:21:10,265 --> 00:21:12,367
It could be a mistake.

488
00:21:12,368 --> 00:21:14,035
Or you could've forgotten about it.

489
00:21:14,036 --> 00:21:16,237
But my top priority right now...

490
00:21:16,238 --> 00:21:18,406
is to eliminate this kind of uncertainty.

491
00:21:18,407 --> 00:21:20,408
But I don't think that's the role
of the Reporter.

492
00:21:20,409 --> 00:21:22,010
It's something we need
to figure out ourselves.

493
00:21:22,011 --> 00:21:23,878
That's when it turns
into yellow journalism.

494
00:21:23,879 --> 00:21:26,214
If we start going, "This person did this,
that person did that."

495
00:21:26,748 --> 00:21:28,348
- Right. That could happen.
- Innocent people...

496
00:21:28,349 --> 00:21:29,950
could die.

497
00:21:29,951 --> 00:21:33,087
Do you mean if the Reporter...

498
00:21:33,188 --> 00:21:36,357
intentionally gave us false information?

499
00:21:36,358 --> 00:21:38,592
Because I don't think...

500
00:21:38,593 --> 00:21:41,629
Nangja would deliberately...

501
00:21:41,630 --> 00:21:43,797
try to hurt someone.

502
00:21:43,798 --> 00:21:45,499
So I think that risk...

503
00:21:45,500 --> 00:21:46,700
is pretty low.

504
00:21:46,701 --> 00:21:49,771
Basically, once we start
relying on people's goodwill,

505
00:21:49,772 --> 00:21:51,005
it's no longer a system.

506
00:21:51,006 --> 00:21:53,073
Then we might as well hope for a dictator.

507
00:21:53,074 --> 00:21:55,910
I think we need to trust the system,
not individuals.

508
00:21:55,911 --> 00:21:57,978
And what you're suggesting now...

509
00:21:57,979 --> 00:22:00,047
depends entirely
on unconditional goodwill.

510
00:22:00,849 --> 00:22:02,817
That's when I got a little pissed off.

511
00:22:02,818 --> 00:22:04,985
Because one thing I can't stand is...

512
00:22:05,120 --> 00:22:06,087
bad manners.

513
00:22:06,622 --> 00:22:08,489
But the fact that he completely
dismissed what I said meant...

514
00:22:08,757 --> 00:22:09,724
First,

515
00:22:10,292 --> 00:22:12,793
he didn't see me as part of the Community.

516
00:22:12,794 --> 00:22:13,761
Second,

517
00:22:13,762 --> 00:22:16,497
he had zero respect for me to begin with.

518
00:22:18,701 --> 00:22:21,001
But this is what makes the media so scary.

519
00:22:21,002 --> 00:22:23,137
"Is the spy male?" "Yes."

520
00:22:23,638 --> 00:22:25,239
Then you ask the next question.

521
00:22:25,240 --> 00:22:26,974
And get,
"I can't give you that information."

522
00:22:27,142 --> 00:22:28,609
Then six people will start
suspecting each other.

523
00:22:28,610 --> 00:22:30,346
I agree, but...

524
00:22:30,347 --> 00:22:34,616
we said the system and how it works
are important in this Community.

525
00:22:34,617 --> 00:22:36,450
The spy is part of that system too.

526
00:22:36,752 --> 00:22:38,820
I think using one of those
six questions on that...

527
00:22:38,821 --> 00:22:41,625
would be in the Community's best interest.

528
00:22:41,626 --> 00:22:43,158
Here's what I think.

529
00:22:43,159 --> 00:22:45,293
The Reporter has a lot of power.

530
00:22:45,294 --> 00:22:47,795
And those questions are
a really valuable resource for us.

531
00:22:47,796 --> 00:22:50,097
Say we go with what Michael suggested,

532
00:22:50,098 --> 00:22:52,567
but it turns out one of the two
just made a mistake.

533
00:22:52,568 --> 00:22:56,638
Then we've used one of our five or so
really important questions...

534
00:22:56,639 --> 00:22:58,572
just to investigate a suspicion
about someone.

535
00:22:58,573 --> 00:23:02,443
Anyway, I think more people here
are worried about the spy...

536
00:23:02,811 --> 00:23:04,545
than you might think.

537
00:23:04,546 --> 00:23:07,114
Really? I'm not so sure.

538
00:23:07,350 --> 00:23:10,852
I actually think that...

539
00:23:10,853 --> 00:23:14,856
constantly speculating
about who the spy might be...

540
00:23:14,857 --> 00:23:18,126
is what's actually spreading more anxiety.

541
00:23:18,127 --> 00:23:20,228
I don't want us jumping to,

542
00:23:20,229 --> 00:23:22,230
"Is this the spy making their move?"

543
00:23:22,231 --> 00:23:23,164
Of course not.

544
00:23:23,165 --> 00:23:25,032
Trying to root out the spy...

545
00:23:25,033 --> 00:23:27,234
is supposed to be very personal.

546
00:23:27,235 --> 00:23:30,171
If the whole point of this Community...

547
00:23:30,172 --> 00:23:31,639
is how well we can come together,

548
00:23:31,640 --> 00:23:34,042
then that's what the Reporter
should focus on too.

549
00:23:34,877 --> 00:23:37,411
He literally looked at me
like I was a cockroach.

550
00:23:37,712 --> 00:23:41,515
I was raising a perfectly valid question
regarding the Community.

551
00:23:42,284 --> 00:23:44,085
And he gave me all that shit for it.

552
00:23:46,855 --> 00:23:49,857
Just one last thing.

553
00:23:51,993 --> 00:23:52,993
After all,

554
00:23:53,729 --> 00:23:55,029
Benjamin is the leader.

555
00:23:55,197 --> 00:23:58,365
He's the one
who's supposed to be leading us.

556
00:23:58,366 --> 00:24:01,569
But honestly, I feel like Superman
and Polar Bear...

557
00:24:01,570 --> 00:24:04,572
are dominating the discussion.

558
00:24:09,811 --> 00:24:11,445
I feel like you two are way too vocal.

559
00:24:11,446 --> 00:24:13,247
If we want different voices in leadership,

560
00:24:13,248 --> 00:24:16,751
we need to hear more from Benjamin.

561
00:24:16,752 --> 00:24:19,553
For days now,
everything has been driven...

562
00:24:19,554 --> 00:24:20,787
by what these two are saying.

563
00:24:20,788 --> 00:24:23,357
I just wanted to be honest about it.

564
00:24:23,358 --> 00:24:24,925
Then challenge what we're saying.

565
00:24:24,926 --> 00:24:26,460
Don't make it about how much we talk.

566
00:24:26,461 --> 00:24:29,162
You speak your mind too, Michael.

567
00:24:34,369 --> 00:24:37,638
When I heard that, I thought,
"Yeah, that's true."

568
00:24:37,639 --> 00:24:39,607
Those two have always had...

569
00:24:39,608 --> 00:24:42,576
such strong voices in the group.

570
00:24:42,577 --> 00:24:45,412
So it was starting to feel like...

571
00:24:45,413 --> 00:24:46,813
everyone else had to choose...

572
00:24:46,814 --> 00:24:49,182
between their two views.

573
00:24:49,183 --> 00:24:52,419
I thought it was brave of him
to call that out.

574
00:24:52,922 --> 00:24:55,222
I mean, that's his opinion,
and I respect it.

575
00:24:55,957 --> 00:25:00,094
But it's also my opinion,
and my choice to keep pushing it.

576
00:25:00,262 --> 00:25:02,496
If that makes me look stubborn,

577
00:25:02,630 --> 00:25:05,799
it's because I need to take
a firm position first.

578
00:25:05,800 --> 00:25:09,603
So the discussion
can move toward something...

579
00:25:09,771 --> 00:25:12,039
more moderate and constructive.

580
00:25:12,040 --> 00:25:15,576
I think the Community can judge that
for themselves.

581
00:25:16,111 --> 00:25:19,079
Okay, I think we can wrap it up like this.

582
00:25:19,080 --> 00:25:21,282
We still have plenty of time
before the Reporter's first task,

583
00:25:21,283 --> 00:25:23,984
since it doesn't happen
until after the evening activities.

584
00:25:23,985 --> 00:25:26,554
We can flesh this out more over dinner.

585
00:25:26,688 --> 00:25:28,855
Okay. Anything related to the Reporter
can be done over dinner.

586
00:25:35,597 --> 00:25:39,567
Here is today's income activity.

587
00:25:40,102 --> 00:25:45,039
Today's income activity
will test your general knowledge.

588
00:25:45,740 --> 00:25:46,773
Someone well-rounded.

589
00:25:47,041 --> 00:25:50,744
The maximum prize for today's
income activity is...

590
00:25:51,279 --> 00:25:53,213
Forty thousand dollars.

591
00:25:53,783 --> 00:25:54,982
Forty thousand.

592
00:25:54,983 --> 00:25:56,317
I guess it increases every day.

593
00:25:56,318 --> 00:25:57,452
Day by day.

594
00:25:57,886 --> 00:26:00,587
First it was 20,000. Then it was 30,000.

595
00:26:00,588 --> 00:26:02,924
For now, the income activity
takes priority.

596
00:26:02,925 --> 00:26:04,358
I think settling that first...

597
00:26:04,359 --> 00:26:06,627
will help us earn more money today.

598
00:26:06,628 --> 00:26:09,697
Honestly, I think everyone has a point.

599
00:26:09,698 --> 00:26:11,899
The reason I haven't said much...

600
00:26:11,900 --> 00:26:15,135
is that I feel like everyone here
is more capable than I am.

601
00:26:15,270 --> 00:26:18,806
But for this,
I think we should prioritize...

602
00:26:18,807 --> 00:26:21,208
whoever's best suited for it.

603
00:26:21,209 --> 00:26:23,210
I'm torn between two things.

604
00:26:23,211 --> 00:26:26,080
First, shouldn't we send the people
who are best at this?

605
00:26:26,081 --> 00:26:29,583
Second, wouldn't it be fair...

606
00:26:29,718 --> 00:26:31,986
to let the people who stayed
behind yesterday go today?

607
00:26:32,354 --> 00:26:34,822
- I'm sensing a divide.
- You're right.

608
00:26:35,056 --> 00:26:38,825
But if everyone who stayed
behind yesterday goes today,

609
00:26:39,294 --> 00:26:41,161
we're just splitting
into the same groups again.

610
00:26:41,162 --> 00:26:45,041
That's what I noticed earlier.

611
00:26:45,066 --> 00:26:46,335
I noticed that too.

612
00:26:46,336 --> 00:26:48,402
It felt too much
like a six-on-six standoff.

613
00:26:48,670 --> 00:26:49,803
We need to mix up the teams.

614
00:26:49,804 --> 00:26:52,840
The people who stay behind
aren't doing the mission.

615
00:26:52,841 --> 00:26:54,074
So naturally,

616
00:26:54,209 --> 00:26:57,678
they end up bonding and getting closer.

617
00:26:57,679 --> 00:26:59,214
But what's unfair is...

618
00:26:59,215 --> 00:27:02,683
the people who stayed behind
yesterday had time to bond.

619
00:27:02,684 --> 00:27:04,084
While the rest of us
didn't get that chance.

620
00:27:04,085 --> 00:27:06,120
- But...
- So what I'm suggesting is...

621
00:27:06,121 --> 00:27:08,355
let's stick with the current split
just for today.

622
00:27:08,356 --> 00:27:09,424
Then tomorrow, let's shuffle the groups,

623
00:27:09,425 --> 00:27:10,858
and assign roles based on ability.

624
00:27:10,859 --> 00:27:13,027
Wouldn't that naturally bring us together?

625
00:27:13,028 --> 00:27:14,496
I disagree.

626
00:27:14,497 --> 00:27:16,130
Can I say something?

627
00:27:16,131 --> 00:27:17,898
You do have a point.

628
00:27:17,899 --> 00:27:20,434
Otherwise, we'll end up
completely divided.

629
00:27:20,435 --> 00:27:23,270
Honestly, if I weren't the reporter,
I'd want to go today.

630
00:27:23,271 --> 00:27:25,773
But if I have to focus on the quiz,

631
00:27:25,774 --> 00:27:28,509
I won't have the bandwidth...

632
00:27:28,777 --> 00:27:30,312
to fulfill my duties as the reporter.

633
00:27:30,313 --> 00:27:31,526
You might miss something important.

634
00:27:31,550 --> 00:27:33,381
So I thought I could stay behind instead.

635
00:27:33,648 --> 00:27:36,116
And someone else
who stayed yesterday could go today.

636
00:27:36,484 --> 00:27:39,319
I thought that might be a good compromise.

637
00:27:39,320 --> 00:27:40,454
That sounds reasonable.

638
00:27:40,455 --> 00:27:41,588
I like that idea.

639
00:27:41,589 --> 00:27:44,057
A 1-5 or 2-4 split could work.

640
00:27:44,058 --> 00:27:45,859
I think we should swap at least two.

641
00:27:45,860 --> 00:27:49,196
Sending two from the other group
isn't a bad idea.

642
00:27:49,197 --> 00:27:50,664
That way, the divide won't be as sharp.

643
00:27:50,665 --> 00:27:52,732
Then let's start with volunteers.

644
00:27:53,101 --> 00:27:54,101
I'd like to go.

645
00:27:54,102 --> 00:27:56,671
My general knowledge is pretty solid.

646
00:27:56,672 --> 00:27:58,138
My memory isn't great, though.

647
00:27:58,740 --> 00:28:01,074
I majored in art.

648
00:28:01,743 --> 00:28:04,344
I'll do whatever I can to help.

649
00:28:04,546 --> 00:28:06,780
I'd love to try it.

650
00:28:06,781 --> 00:28:08,015
Sounds good.

651
00:28:08,016 --> 00:28:10,718
I want to experience
the mission firsthand too.

652
00:28:11,152 --> 00:28:12,719
We just need one more.

653
00:28:12,720 --> 00:28:13,920
- One more spot.
- Just one, right?

654
00:28:14,155 --> 00:28:15,722
I think Michael would be great.

655
00:28:15,723 --> 00:28:18,291
Michael has experience
in so many different fields.

656
00:28:20,462 --> 00:28:21,629
You should go, Michael.

657
00:28:21,896 --> 00:28:22,929
All right, I'll go.

658
00:28:23,531 --> 00:28:25,699
Honestly, I don't really want to go.

659
00:28:25,700 --> 00:28:28,602
We still have
so many unresolved conflicts.

660
00:28:28,603 --> 00:28:31,471
Going like this
will only deepen the divide.

661
00:28:31,472 --> 00:28:34,374
I think that's an assumption on your part.

662
00:28:34,375 --> 00:28:37,249
By that logic, you're assuming
the six people staying here...

663
00:28:37,274 --> 00:28:39,179
completely trust one another.

664
00:28:39,180 --> 00:28:40,347
- Not at all.
- And besides...

665
00:28:40,348 --> 00:28:42,516
Just because the group going today
talked yesterday,

666
00:28:42,517 --> 00:28:45,452
doesn't mean they're in an alliance
or have absolute trust.

667
00:28:45,453 --> 00:28:47,454
I'm only bringing this up for one reason.

668
00:28:47,689 --> 00:28:49,390
It's because I still feel unsettled.

669
00:28:49,391 --> 00:28:51,659
We talked for over two hours yesterday.

670
00:28:51,660 --> 00:28:52,993
And again this morning.

671
00:28:52,994 --> 00:28:54,628
Yet you keep saying you can't trust me.

672
00:28:54,629 --> 00:28:56,396
And you keep looking at me with suspicion.

673
00:28:56,397 --> 00:28:57,498
Okay, hold on.

674
00:28:57,499 --> 00:29:00,067
- I'm not saying I don't trust you.
- Michael, relax.

675
00:29:01,202 --> 00:29:02,502
Just take a breath.

676
00:29:03,138 --> 00:29:04,772
I get that we see things differently.

677
00:29:05,874 --> 00:29:08,709
Everyone kept telling me to calm down.

678
00:29:08,710 --> 00:29:10,177
That's when it hit me.

679
00:29:10,178 --> 00:29:12,713
I'm screwed. This looks bad.

680
00:29:12,714 --> 00:29:16,250
At this rate, they're going
to frame me as the spy.

681
00:29:16,251 --> 00:29:18,319
And I could actually end up ostracized
from the community.

682
00:29:18,720 --> 00:29:21,955
The tension between them was palpable.

683
00:29:22,023 --> 00:29:25,092
He just kept obsessing over the spy.

684
00:29:25,093 --> 00:29:28,996
It felt like he was deliberately
trying to stir things up.

685
00:29:29,130 --> 00:29:30,698
So who do we have on the team so far?

686
00:29:30,699 --> 00:29:31,964
Could you read off the names?

687
00:29:31,965 --> 00:29:33,834
Hippo, Sugar.

688
00:29:34,135 --> 00:29:36,636
Dark Knight, Genie, Michael.

689
00:29:37,238 --> 00:29:40,040
I still honestly think
there needs to be one person...

690
00:29:40,041 --> 00:29:41,642
from the other camp to go with us.

691
00:29:41,643 --> 00:29:44,344
Then we need
to narrow down the volunteers.

692
00:29:44,345 --> 00:29:46,280
So you're proposing a 2-4 split?

693
00:29:46,281 --> 00:29:47,348
Yeah, two and four.

694
00:29:48,983 --> 00:29:50,450
Those who went yesterday, four who didn't.

695
00:29:50,451 --> 00:29:52,152
I get why you might feel that way.

696
00:29:52,153 --> 00:29:53,987
But if we keep phrasing it like that,

697
00:29:53,988 --> 00:29:55,522
we're acting like we're
already factionalized.

698
00:29:55,523 --> 00:29:58,591
No, I don't see it that way.

699
00:29:58,592 --> 00:30:00,060
It's totally fine to just go like this.

700
00:30:00,061 --> 00:30:01,828
We're just going out to make some money.

701
00:30:01,829 --> 00:30:04,331
Let's not overthink it.

702
00:30:04,332 --> 00:30:07,067
It feels terrible
being singled out like this.

703
00:30:07,068 --> 00:30:08,069
I don't know how it came to this.

704
00:30:08,070 --> 00:30:09,636
It shouldn't be this complicated.

705
00:30:09,637 --> 00:30:11,504
Those who went yesterday will stay behind.

706
00:30:11,505 --> 00:30:14,741
And those who stayed yesterday
will go today.

707
00:30:14,742 --> 00:30:16,710
We can mix things up tomorrow.

708
00:30:16,711 --> 00:30:18,611
Fair enough.

709
00:30:20,949 --> 00:30:23,049
I really hate this kind of vibe.

710
00:30:23,074 --> 00:30:26,844
Hate to say it,
but this feels like such a girl fight.

711
00:30:27,489 --> 00:30:31,792
To be honest,
when I was talking about the spy,

712
00:30:31,793 --> 00:30:35,029
that is definitely a crucial part
of the community, right?

713
00:30:35,030 --> 00:30:37,197
- That is right.
- I feel threatened.

714
00:30:37,198 --> 00:30:40,534
It's just freedom of opinion, right?

715
00:30:40,535 --> 00:30:44,171
And people are able to have
their opinions respected.

716
00:30:44,506 --> 00:30:46,373
But I just did not feel respected.

717
00:30:46,374 --> 00:30:49,009
Quite a few people
actually feel threatened...

718
00:30:49,010 --> 00:30:50,512
by his presence.

719
00:30:50,513 --> 00:30:54,648
I think this is just a mild confrontation.

720
00:30:54,649 --> 00:30:55,850
- Yeah, very mild.
- Yeah.

721
00:30:56,151 --> 00:30:58,752
I have mixed feelings about Michael.

722
00:30:58,953 --> 00:31:01,855
Honestly, I love seeing him
butt heads with people.

723
00:31:01,923 --> 00:31:03,990
So part of me is really grateful to him.

724
00:31:03,991 --> 00:31:05,425
I appreciate that side of him.

725
00:31:05,660 --> 00:31:07,528
But I really wish
he'd stop speaking English to me.

726
00:31:08,969 --> 00:31:10,164
It stresses me out.

727
00:31:10,498 --> 00:31:12,666
Can I borrow you for 30 seconds, Michael?

728
00:31:13,835 --> 00:31:15,836
- Hey, Gray.
- What made you get so heated?

729
00:31:15,837 --> 00:31:19,005
I really don't see eye to eye...

730
00:31:19,006 --> 00:31:21,275
with Superman.

731
00:31:21,643 --> 00:31:23,110
He's too overbearing.

732
00:31:23,111 --> 00:31:24,312
And honestly,

733
00:31:24,337 --> 00:31:27,782
this is what really rubbed me
the wrong way.

734
00:31:27,783 --> 00:31:29,416
It's "The Community," right?

735
00:31:29,417 --> 00:31:30,951
It's not "Their Community."

736
00:31:30,952 --> 00:31:33,721
But it feels
like it's Superman's community.

737
00:31:33,722 --> 00:31:35,299
I get where he's coming from, though.

738
00:31:35,324 --> 00:31:38,091
He's done a bunch
of survival shows before.

739
00:31:38,092 --> 00:31:39,660
Those smoke breaks can get dangerous.

740
00:31:39,661 --> 00:31:41,962
I've actually started noticing
a clear divide between...

741
00:31:41,963 --> 00:31:43,764
- the smokers and nonsmokers.
- Exactly.

742
00:31:43,765 --> 00:31:46,767
Anyone who stirs up division
will look like a threat.

743
00:31:46,768 --> 00:31:48,236
I completely get that.

744
00:31:48,237 --> 00:31:50,470
But if you let your emotions
flare up like that,

745
00:31:50,471 --> 00:31:52,773
people will hesitate...

746
00:31:52,774 --> 00:31:54,676
to team up with you.

747
00:31:54,677 --> 00:31:57,077
And that will only work against you.

748
00:31:57,214 --> 00:32:00,079
My concern is
that everyone is feeling on edge.

749
00:32:00,080 --> 00:32:02,682
Go over, reassure him,
and smooth things over, man.

750
00:32:02,683 --> 00:32:04,250
Even the casual way they say "man."

751
00:32:04,251 --> 00:32:06,419
It builds camaraderie so fast.

752
00:32:06,420 --> 00:32:08,555
The smoking, the drinking,
it brings them together so effortlessly.

753
00:32:08,556 --> 00:32:10,090
That part bothers me a little too.

754
00:32:10,091 --> 00:32:12,660
If we aren't careful,
this will turn into men versus women.

755
00:32:15,230 --> 00:32:16,864
Why is everyone fighting so much?

756
00:32:17,765 --> 00:32:19,667
Everyone here is great.

757
00:32:21,302 --> 00:32:23,670
(Mission participants)

758
00:32:24,672 --> 00:32:26,272
This rift is only going to get wider.

759
00:32:26,273 --> 00:32:29,376
This is what happens when you
leave people to their own devices.

760
00:32:30,645 --> 00:32:32,080
I honestly don't get
why they're doing this.

761
00:32:36,284 --> 00:32:38,007
The participants
for today's income-earning activity...

762
00:32:38,031 --> 00:32:38,886
will now be announced.

763
00:32:38,887 --> 00:32:39,920
Go make us some bank.

764
00:32:39,921 --> 00:32:41,255
- Will do.
- You got this.

765
00:32:41,256 --> 00:32:42,422
- Let's go.
- Let's go!

766
00:32:42,423 --> 00:32:43,957
- We'll bring back a fortune.
- Promise?

767
00:32:43,958 --> 00:32:45,326
I know they'll do great.

768
00:32:45,660 --> 00:32:47,127
- Let's do this!
- Yeah!

769
00:32:47,128 --> 00:32:49,162
(The mission team heads out.)

770
00:32:49,163 --> 00:32:50,897
I hope we make more than 20,000.

771
00:32:50,898 --> 00:32:53,033
Let's be ambitious and aim for 24,000!

772
00:32:53,902 --> 00:32:55,635
Let's make everyone proud.

773
00:32:55,636 --> 00:32:56,870
(The mission team arrives at the venue.)

774
00:32:57,772 --> 00:32:59,207
Looks like there's a time limit.

775
00:33:00,642 --> 00:33:04,177
Welcome to the income-earning activity.

776
00:33:04,746 --> 00:33:06,213
Today's mission is...

777
00:33:06,782 --> 00:33:08,882
"Memory Messenger."

778
00:33:08,883 --> 00:33:09,783
Memory Messenger?

779
00:33:10,251 --> 00:33:13,186
I think there was even a book
about memory techniques by that name.

780
00:33:13,187 --> 00:33:14,187
Yeah.

781
00:33:15,423 --> 00:33:17,391
(How to Remember without Memorizing)

782
00:33:17,492 --> 00:33:21,829
Here are the rules for Memory Messenger.

783
00:33:22,864 --> 00:33:26,133
First, the Messenger
must review the material...

784
00:33:26,134 --> 00:33:28,101
within the given time limit...

785
00:33:28,102 --> 00:33:31,271
and explain it
to help the residents memorize it.

786
00:33:32,140 --> 00:33:35,676
Second, once the briefing time expires,

787
00:33:35,677 --> 00:33:37,411
the Messenger returns
to the Memory Room...

788
00:33:37,412 --> 00:33:39,746
and cannot take part in the quiz.

789
00:33:40,248 --> 00:33:41,715
The Messenger's role is crucial,

790
00:33:41,716 --> 00:33:43,451
- but so is the role of the rest.
- Definitely.

791
00:33:43,852 --> 00:33:48,255
Third, each round consists
of 10 questions.

792
00:33:48,656 --> 00:33:51,925
Each correct answer earns 500 dollars.

793
00:33:51,926 --> 00:33:55,962
If you answer all 10 correctly
without a single mistake,

794
00:33:55,963 --> 00:33:58,598
you will receive a perfect score bonus
of 10,000 dollars.

795
00:33:58,866 --> 00:34:01,268
Getting a clean sweep is key.

796
00:34:01,269 --> 00:34:02,302
Fourth,

797
00:34:02,303 --> 00:34:05,771
during the quiz, you may spend 500 dollars
from the communal fund...

798
00:34:05,772 --> 00:34:08,975
to review the material for one minute.

799
00:34:12,213 --> 00:34:16,950
Round one of Memory Messenger begins now.

800
00:34:17,285 --> 00:34:19,987
Messenger, please select a topic box...

801
00:34:19,988 --> 00:34:22,155
and proceed to the Memory Room.

802
00:34:22,457 --> 00:34:24,191
Looks like we get to pick.

803
00:34:27,328 --> 00:34:28,728
Are there different categories?

804
00:34:28,729 --> 00:34:29,729
What are the options?

805
00:34:30,031 --> 00:34:31,064
Baek Seok?

806
00:34:31,065 --> 00:34:33,166
We might have to memorize a poem.

807
00:34:33,167 --> 00:34:34,901
I think this one's philosophy.

808
00:34:34,902 --> 00:34:36,303
I know a bit about that.

809
00:34:36,304 --> 00:34:37,404
And this one...

810
00:34:37,405 --> 00:34:38,440
Sounds like a historical event.

811
00:34:38,441 --> 00:34:41,108
Is anyone familiar with Bruegel?

812
00:34:41,109 --> 00:34:42,576
Should I be the Messenger?

813
00:34:42,577 --> 00:34:44,644
- Want to take on Bruegel?
- Sure.

814
00:34:44,645 --> 00:34:45,946
I'll give it my all.

815
00:34:46,047 --> 00:34:47,782
- You got this!
- Go for it!

816
00:34:47,783 --> 00:34:48,749
(Round 1: Bruegel, Messenger: Sugar)

817
00:34:48,750 --> 00:34:49,750
I think she'll crush it.

818
00:34:50,551 --> 00:34:52,685
- Please remove your blindfold.
- Okay.

819
00:34:56,023 --> 00:34:59,869
("Netherlandish Proverbs"
by Pieter Bruegel)

820
00:34:59,894 --> 00:35:01,261
Oh, man.

821
00:35:01,291 --> 00:35:17,718
(Depicts over 100 Dutch proverbs
in a single painting)

822
00:35:20,248 --> 00:35:23,016
(Sugar's Challenge: 58 Proverbs)

823
00:35:23,785 --> 00:35:26,387
(Filling the well
after the calf has drowned.)

824
00:35:26,388 --> 00:35:28,589
(Meaning: Taking action
when it is already too late)

825
00:35:29,490 --> 00:35:32,125
(Catching fish in someone else's net)

826
00:35:32,126 --> 00:35:34,194
(Meaning: Taking credit
for someone else's hard work)

827
00:35:35,663 --> 00:35:37,964
(Warming yourself by a burning house)

828
00:35:37,965 --> 00:35:40,867
(Meaning: Profiting off disaster
with no regard for others)

829
00:35:43,137 --> 00:35:46,306
(Shooting a second arrow
to find the first)

830
00:35:46,307 --> 00:35:49,375
(Meaning: Repeating
the exact same foolish mistake)

831
00:35:50,578 --> 00:35:52,178
I majored in fine arts.

832
00:35:52,179 --> 00:35:55,048
So I went in feeling pretty confident.

833
00:35:55,516 --> 00:35:58,151
But when I saw there were 58 proverbs,

834
00:35:58,152 --> 00:36:00,720
my mind went blank.

835
00:36:01,756 --> 00:36:04,024
I just put my head down and crammed...

836
00:36:04,025 --> 00:36:05,893
the way I used to for school exams.

837
00:36:16,771 --> 00:36:18,471
- I'm back.
- Great job.

838
00:36:20,408 --> 00:36:22,609
I have 15 minutes to brief you all.

839
00:36:22,610 --> 00:36:24,077
It's a painting.

840
00:36:24,078 --> 00:36:27,481
- I memorized around 58 proverbs.
- Fifty-eight? Wow.

841
00:36:29,517 --> 00:36:31,651
(No study materials or writing tools...)

842
00:36:31,652 --> 00:36:33,687
(are permitted outside the Memory Room.)

843
00:36:34,956 --> 00:36:37,691
Messenger, you now have 15 minutes...

844
00:36:37,992 --> 00:36:41,328
to explain the material to the residents.

845
00:36:41,929 --> 00:36:43,863
See this broomstick?

846
00:36:43,864 --> 00:36:46,299
A couple is kissing under it.

847
00:36:46,300 --> 00:36:48,534
They're getting married, but in secret.

848
00:36:48,535 --> 00:36:51,171
It means they're having an affair.

849
00:36:51,172 --> 00:36:54,307
Here, you see a jester holding a card.

850
00:36:54,308 --> 00:36:56,576
When a fool gets dealt a good hand,

851
00:36:56,577 --> 00:37:00,114
it's purely down to dumb luck.

852
00:37:00,614 --> 00:37:02,916
These two are tweaking each other's noses.

853
00:37:02,917 --> 00:37:05,652
The nose can symbolize deceit.

854
00:37:05,653 --> 00:37:06,986
They're fooling one another.

855
00:37:07,388 --> 00:37:10,022
These cards are falling,

856
00:37:10,023 --> 00:37:12,225
and picking one up means...

857
00:37:12,226 --> 00:37:14,728
you're leaving things to chance.

858
00:37:14,729 --> 00:37:17,097
This man is banging his head
against a brick wall.

859
00:37:17,098 --> 00:37:18,431
It's utterly futile.

860
00:37:18,432 --> 00:37:19,499
- A waste of effort.
- A waste of time.

861
00:37:19,500 --> 00:37:21,868
A cat won't just sit still. It bolts.

862
00:37:21,869 --> 00:37:24,737
So trying to bell the cat...

863
00:37:24,738 --> 00:37:26,173
is bound to fail.

864
00:37:26,340 --> 00:37:29,642
This man is bowing his head...

865
00:37:29,643 --> 00:37:31,077
to crawl inside this globe.

866
00:37:31,412 --> 00:37:35,848
He knows when to swallow his pride
to get ahead.

867
00:37:35,849 --> 00:37:36,647
And look at this.

868
00:37:36,671 --> 00:37:39,619
The world turned upside down.
It means the world has gone mad.

869
00:37:39,620 --> 00:37:41,920
Here's the crane and the fox.

870
00:37:41,921 --> 00:37:43,423
Just like in the fable,

871
00:37:43,424 --> 00:37:45,391
they're each looking out
only for themselves.

872
00:37:45,392 --> 00:37:48,294
In the end, they both get
a taste of their own medicine.

873
00:37:48,295 --> 00:37:51,097
This man has the globe
resting on his palm.

874
00:37:51,098 --> 00:37:53,466
He holds the world in his hand.

875
00:37:53,467 --> 00:37:55,435
It means having everything
lined up in your favor.

876
00:37:55,436 --> 00:37:58,204
This is spilled water.
No use crying over spilled milk.

877
00:37:58,882 --> 00:38:01,417
This man is carrying a lantern
looking for an ax.

878
00:38:01,442 --> 00:38:04,410
It means gathering the tools
to put your skills to work.

879
00:38:04,644 --> 00:38:07,747
Here's an old woman fleeing a fire.

880
00:38:07,748 --> 00:38:09,582
"Fear can make even an old woman run."

881
00:38:09,895 --> 00:38:11,683
That's the meaning behind it.

882
00:38:11,684 --> 00:38:14,087
Someone else's house is ablaze,

883
00:38:14,088 --> 00:38:15,790
yet he's warming himself by the fire.

884
00:38:16,190 --> 00:38:17,190
He's taking advantage
of another's misfortune.

885
00:38:17,191 --> 00:38:18,491
That's the devil.

886
00:38:18,492 --> 00:38:20,160
He's confessing to the devil.

887
00:38:20,161 --> 00:38:22,529
It means leaking secrets to the enemy.

888
00:38:22,863 --> 00:38:23,830
I see.

889
00:38:24,231 --> 00:38:28,368
Sugar was just rattling them off
with total confidence.

890
00:38:28,369 --> 00:38:30,170
I had no idea she was
such an articulate speaker.

891
00:38:30,171 --> 00:38:31,572
I saw her in a whole new light.

892
00:38:31,739 --> 00:38:33,640
It caught me completely off guard.

893
00:38:33,641 --> 00:38:35,341
She's seriously capable.

894
00:38:35,342 --> 00:38:38,278
How on earth did she memorize all of that?

895
00:38:38,279 --> 00:38:39,979
And she explained
every single one so clearly.

896
00:38:39,980 --> 00:38:41,348
I was genuinely blown away.

897
00:38:41,500 --> 00:38:43,017
I found that side of her
really attractive.

898
00:38:43,317 --> 00:38:45,184
Did you say this sword
symbolizes a challenge?

899
00:38:45,185 --> 00:38:46,185
A blade without an edge.

900
00:38:46,186 --> 00:38:47,221
It's useless.

901
00:38:47,222 --> 00:38:48,888
Time is up.

902
00:38:49,757 --> 00:38:53,261
Messenger, please return
to the Memory Room immediately.

903
00:38:53,262 --> 00:38:55,162
I have no idea what they'll ask.

904
00:38:55,163 --> 00:38:56,830
Well done!

905
00:38:56,831 --> 00:38:59,499
(The Messenger cannot take part
in the quiz.)

906
00:38:59,500 --> 00:39:01,802
The round one quiz of Memory Messenger...

907
00:39:01,937 --> 00:39:04,304
- begins now.
- Let's do this.

908
00:39:04,305 --> 00:39:05,472
(Round 1 quiz begins.)

909
00:39:05,473 --> 00:39:06,406
(Bruegel)

910
00:39:07,117 --> 00:39:10,019
"What is the object above the couple
kissing by the window?"

911
00:39:10,044 --> 00:39:11,645
(What is the object above the couple
kissing by the window?)

912
00:39:11,646 --> 00:39:13,347
See this broomstick?

913
00:39:13,581 --> 00:39:15,915
A couple is kissing under it.

914
00:39:16,083 --> 00:39:18,584
It means they're having an affair.

915
00:39:19,120 --> 00:39:21,021
Oh, that one! A broomstick.

916
00:39:21,022 --> 00:39:21,888
- Broomstick.
- A broomstick!

917
00:39:21,889 --> 00:39:22,989
Correct.

918
00:39:23,491 --> 00:39:25,992
You have earned 500 dollars.

919
00:39:28,129 --> 00:39:31,129
(What is the man sprawled on the plank
trying to reach?)

920
00:39:32,734 --> 00:39:35,035
There's a loaf of bread on either side,

921
00:39:35,036 --> 00:39:37,270
yet he can't reach either one.

922
00:39:37,705 --> 00:39:38,705
Ah, bread!

923
00:39:39,306 --> 00:39:40,339
Correct.

924
00:39:40,508 --> 00:39:41,441
Nice.

925
00:39:41,442 --> 00:39:44,244
- You have earned 500 dollars.
- We've got this.

926
00:39:44,612 --> 00:39:46,958
(What is the man holding a lantern
under the plank searching for?)

927
00:39:47,848 --> 00:39:50,250
This one's carrying a lantern,
looking for an ax.

928
00:39:50,251 --> 00:39:52,219
- I think it was an ax.
- An ax.

929
00:39:52,220 --> 00:39:53,486
- That's it.
- An ax.

930
00:39:53,487 --> 00:39:54,487
An ax!

931
00:39:54,789 --> 00:39:55,789
Correct.

932
00:39:55,790 --> 00:39:56,890
She really did an incredible job.

933
00:39:56,891 --> 00:39:59,426
You have earned 500 dollars.

934
00:40:00,494 --> 00:40:02,649
(Which foot is bare
on the man banging his head...)

935
00:40:02,673 --> 00:40:04,240
(against the wall while holding a knife?)

936
00:40:04,265 --> 00:40:06,666
- Which foot was bare?
- Left or right?

937
00:40:06,667 --> 00:40:09,069
This man is banging his head
against a brick wall.

938
00:40:09,070 --> 00:40:11,204
It's utterly futile. A waste of effort.

939
00:40:11,205 --> 00:40:13,842
If he's facing that way,
it could be his right foot.

940
00:40:13,843 --> 00:40:15,075
I'm pretty sure it was the right one.

941
00:40:15,076 --> 00:40:16,443
Let's buy a hint just to play it safe.

942
00:40:16,444 --> 00:40:18,513
Isn't it a 50-50 shot, though?

943
00:40:18,514 --> 00:40:20,313
Those are decent odds.

944
00:40:20,314 --> 00:40:21,781
Let's just be sure.

945
00:40:22,049 --> 00:40:24,184
No, I don't think we should burn a hint.

946
00:40:24,185 --> 00:40:25,252
It's a pure coin toss anyway.

947
00:40:25,253 --> 00:40:26,586
A 50-50?

948
00:40:26,587 --> 00:40:29,122
We need to be conservative with our hints.

949
00:40:29,123 --> 00:40:31,357
He's holding a knife, banging his head.

950
00:40:31,826 --> 00:40:33,660
Feet usually line up like this.

951
00:40:33,661 --> 00:40:35,462
We should've paid attention
to that detail.

952
00:40:35,463 --> 00:40:36,763
Yeah, usually like this.

953
00:40:36,764 --> 00:40:38,132
If one was barefoot,

954
00:40:38,699 --> 00:40:40,633
it had to be the right.

955
00:40:40,634 --> 00:40:42,435
- Should we lock in right?
- Yeah.

956
00:40:42,603 --> 00:40:43,670
The right foot.

957
00:40:45,873 --> 00:40:46,873
Incorrect.

958
00:40:49,343 --> 00:40:50,610
We gave it our best shot.

959
00:40:50,811 --> 00:40:52,478
(What is in the mouth
of the person belling the cat?)

960
00:40:52,479 --> 00:40:53,513
What was in his mouth?

961
00:40:53,514 --> 00:40:55,049
- I remember something sticking out.
- A feather?

962
00:40:56,917 --> 00:40:57,850
A feather.

963
00:40:58,152 --> 00:40:59,252
Incorrect.

964
00:40:59,253 --> 00:41:00,420
A knife!

965
00:41:00,421 --> 00:41:01,677
(What is the person scooping
up spilled porridge...)

966
00:41:01,701 --> 00:41:02,422
(doing with their other hand?)

967
00:41:02,423 --> 00:41:03,423
What was he doing?

968
00:41:03,491 --> 00:41:04,558
Spilled porridge.

969
00:41:05,926 --> 00:41:07,493
He's holding the bucket.

970
00:41:07,762 --> 00:41:08,762
Incorrect.

971
00:41:08,787 --> 00:41:09,371
(The head)

972
00:41:09,396 --> 00:41:10,629
That's what he was doing.

973
00:41:10,630 --> 00:41:12,031
Right.

974
00:41:12,032 --> 00:41:14,800
(How many hearts are on the cards
scattered on the roof?)

975
00:41:14,801 --> 00:41:16,070
The number of hearts on the cards.

976
00:41:16,699 --> 00:41:17,471
Wait.

977
00:41:17,671 --> 00:41:19,605
These cards are falling,

978
00:41:20,041 --> 00:41:22,041
and picking one up means...

979
00:41:28,115 --> 00:41:29,048
Seven!

980
00:41:31,485 --> 00:41:32,585
Correct.

981
00:41:33,587 --> 00:41:36,055
- You have earned 500 dollars.
- Damn, Genie!

982
00:41:36,056 --> 00:41:38,057
- That was clutch.
- Insane.

983
00:41:38,058 --> 00:41:40,059
Even I thought the card question
was brutal.

984
00:41:40,060 --> 00:41:42,964
Nailing that felt amazing.

985
00:41:42,965 --> 00:41:45,632
I was so proud of myself.

986
00:41:45,633 --> 00:41:46,866
(How many chickens are
in front of the man...)

987
00:41:46,867 --> 00:41:48,334
(feeling the hen's belly?)

988
00:41:48,335 --> 00:41:49,802
- It was three.
- Yeah.

989
00:41:49,803 --> 00:41:50,670
Three.

990
00:41:50,871 --> 00:41:51,871
Correct.

991
00:41:52,807 --> 00:41:54,474
You have earned 500 dollars.

992
00:41:54,499 --> 00:41:56,335
How can you be so good?

993
00:41:56,577 --> 00:41:57,777
(In which hand is the man
holding the fan...)

994
00:41:57,778 --> 00:41:58,845
(as he tries to shield
the water from the light?)

995
00:41:58,846 --> 00:41:59,746
His left hand.

996
00:42:00,214 --> 00:42:01,347
Correct.

997
00:42:01,348 --> 00:42:03,750
You have earned 500 dollars.

998
00:42:03,751 --> 00:42:04,751
(How many globes representing
the world appear in the painting?)

999
00:42:04,752 --> 00:42:05,886
The upside-down one.

1000
00:42:05,911 --> 00:42:08,821
The one the believer is holding.

1001
00:42:08,822 --> 00:42:10,523
Was that man ducking into a globe as well?

1002
00:42:10,524 --> 00:42:12,293
- That one too.
- Four!

1003
00:42:13,494 --> 00:42:14,561
Correct.

1004
00:42:14,662 --> 00:42:17,263
You have earned 500 dollars.

1005
00:42:17,264 --> 00:42:19,299
That concludes the round.

1006
00:42:20,067 --> 00:42:24,371
In round one, you earned
a total of 3,500 dollars.

1007
00:42:24,372 --> 00:42:26,039
- We crushed that.
- We did.

1008
00:42:26,807 --> 00:42:28,575
Our Messenger carried us.

1009
00:42:28,576 --> 00:42:30,476
- Seven right!
- Did you get them?

1010
00:42:30,477 --> 00:42:32,912
They even asked how many hearts
were on the cards.

1011
00:42:32,913 --> 00:42:34,347
And we nailed it.

1012
00:42:34,348 --> 00:42:36,949
- I got that one. Can you believe it?
- Incredible.

1013
00:42:37,651 --> 00:42:42,122
Round two of Memory Messenger begins now.

1014
00:42:42,123 --> 00:42:44,667
- Next up.
- Who's going next?

1015
00:42:44,692 --> 00:42:46,359
I think it should be Michael.

1016
00:42:46,360 --> 00:42:47,260
Sounds good.

1017
00:42:48,262 --> 00:42:49,796
(Baek Seok, ROME, 1994.07.08, Bruegel)

1018
00:42:50,664 --> 00:42:52,164
I'll take this one.

1019
00:42:52,566 --> 00:42:53,533
All right.

1020
00:42:54,201 --> 00:42:55,468
- You've got this, Michael.
- Yeah.

1021
00:42:55,469 --> 00:42:57,670
- Go get them, Michael!
- Let's go!

1022
00:42:57,671 --> 00:42:58,538
Do it!

1023
00:42:58,539 --> 00:42:59,372
(1994.07.08,
Memory Messenger TIME: 20 minutes)

1024
00:42:59,373 --> 00:43:01,208
Please remove your blindfold.

1025
00:43:04,879 --> 00:43:06,746
(Article on the Death
of North Korean Leader Kim Il Sung)

1026
00:43:06,747 --> 00:43:08,682
(Kim Il Sung Dead)

1027
00:43:08,683 --> 00:43:11,718
(The Dong-A Ilbo,
July 10, 1994, July 24, 1994)

1028
00:43:13,153 --> 00:43:15,354
I couldn't have picked a worse topic.

1029
00:43:15,522 --> 00:43:16,999
It was packed with Chinese characters,

1030
00:43:17,024 --> 00:43:18,826
and I'd never read
a vintage newspaper before.

1031
00:43:19,293 --> 00:43:20,526
At first glance,

1032
00:43:20,527 --> 00:43:22,127
I thought, "All right, history."

1033
00:43:22,128 --> 00:43:24,764
Then I looked closer and went,
"Wait, why can't I read any of this?"

1034
00:43:24,765 --> 00:43:26,432
Oh, man.

1035
00:43:26,433 --> 00:43:29,435
Then I realized it was printed vertically.

1036
00:43:29,737 --> 00:43:30,838
That helped make sense of it.

1037
00:43:31,171 --> 00:43:32,940
Then I moved to the next line,
and it made no sense again.

1038
00:43:33,173 --> 00:43:36,051
I genuinely began questioning
if my Korean was really that terrible.

1039
00:43:36,076 --> 00:43:37,409
It was completely baffling.

1040
00:43:37,645 --> 00:43:39,146
How on earth do I even explain this?

1041
00:43:39,380 --> 00:43:40,547
What threw me off was...

1042
00:43:40,781 --> 00:43:42,148
the text was split into columns.

1043
00:43:42,149 --> 00:43:45,351
Once I realized that, I started
reading column by column.

1044
00:43:45,653 --> 00:43:48,755
Reading through everything was impossible,
and I couldn't decipher the Hanja anyway.

1045
00:43:48,756 --> 00:43:53,059
So I shifted strategy to grasp
the general gist of each section.

1046
00:43:53,060 --> 00:43:54,395
I figured the smartest play...

1047
00:43:54,420 --> 00:43:57,565
was to map out where each headline
and theme sat on the page.

1048
00:43:57,566 --> 00:43:58,932
That became my entire focus.

1049
00:44:02,569 --> 00:44:04,503
Here's my take.

1050
00:44:04,638 --> 00:44:07,707
On day one,
based on everyone's goodwill...

1051
00:44:07,708 --> 00:44:09,875
we tried to divide the pot evenly.

1052
00:44:09,876 --> 00:44:12,411
Even so, a few people walked away
with more than the rest.

1053
00:44:12,412 --> 00:44:15,581
I see the attack token
almost like a nuclear deterrent.

1054
00:44:15,582 --> 00:44:17,817
It keeps everyone in check. But...

1055
00:44:17,818 --> 00:44:19,151
Mutually assured destruction.

1056
00:44:19,152 --> 00:44:20,953
If everyone hoards it
until the bitter end,

1057
00:44:20,954 --> 00:44:22,688
we all go broke together.

1058
00:44:22,990 --> 00:44:24,057
Someone has to fire it.

1059
00:44:24,058 --> 00:44:25,992
Eventually, someone
has to pull the trigger.

1060
00:44:25,993 --> 00:44:27,494
If you don't want to swallow
a 5,000-dollar penalty,

1061
00:44:27,495 --> 00:44:28,295
you have to act.

1062
00:44:28,320 --> 00:44:29,829
Which means someone
has to take the first shot.

1063
00:44:29,830 --> 00:44:32,398
Or we take the leap,
assume everyone acts in good faith,

1064
00:44:32,399 --> 00:44:35,068
and mutually agree to neutralize
the whole thing.

1065
00:44:35,069 --> 00:44:36,569
Basically, wipe it off the board entirely.

1066
00:44:36,570 --> 00:44:38,304
It's already up to 2,000 dollars today.

1067
00:44:38,472 --> 00:44:40,406
Before it climbs any further,
we could find a loophole...

1068
00:44:40,407 --> 00:44:41,808
to outsmart the game and neutralize it.

1069
00:44:41,809 --> 00:44:44,544
- The ideology checks?
- Yeah, get rid of them.

1070
00:44:44,545 --> 00:44:46,512
We could talk through that.

1071
00:44:46,513 --> 00:44:49,217
Though that feels
a bit too coercive to me.

1072
00:44:49,218 --> 00:44:50,917
- True.
- There might...

1073
00:44:51,118 --> 00:44:53,519
genuinely be folks here
who just came to earn prize money.

1074
00:44:53,520 --> 00:44:55,588
It starts bordering on totalitarianism.

1075
00:44:55,589 --> 00:44:57,124
That brings its own risks.

1076
00:44:57,858 --> 00:44:59,225
For all we know,
there might not even be a spy.

1077
00:44:59,226 --> 00:45:01,427
That's just it.
I keep wondering if a spy even exists.

1078
00:45:01,428 --> 00:45:03,095
If I were cast as the spy,

1079
00:45:03,096 --> 00:45:04,330
I wonder what benefit I could get.

1080
00:45:04,331 --> 00:45:06,934
Are they trying to get us
to invent a spy in our heads?

1081
00:45:10,438 --> 00:45:11,838
That was where I struggled.

1082
00:45:12,106 --> 00:45:13,773
That's the tricky part.

1083
00:45:13,774 --> 00:45:15,309
Shall we begin?

1084
00:45:15,943 --> 00:45:19,112
The round two quiz starts now.

1085
00:45:19,113 --> 00:45:20,546
Let me lay out the big picture first.

1086
00:45:20,547 --> 00:45:22,749
Then you guys can divide
and conquer the details.

1087
00:45:22,750 --> 00:45:23,683
He passed away, right?

1088
00:45:23,684 --> 00:45:25,551
This section covers the nuclear crisis.

1089
00:45:25,552 --> 00:45:27,320
And down here is
the National Meteorological Agency.

1090
00:45:27,321 --> 00:45:28,588
(How many Korean citizens did
President Kim Young Sam vow to protect?)

1091
00:45:28,589 --> 00:45:29,489
Seventy million.

1092
00:45:29,490 --> 00:45:30,556
Correct.

1093
00:45:30,858 --> 00:45:33,326
- Was it 82? 83?
- It was 83.

1094
00:45:33,327 --> 00:45:34,160
Incorrect.

1095
00:45:35,229 --> 00:45:36,262
So close!

1096
00:45:36,263 --> 00:45:38,665
The chief US delegate who visited Geneva.

1097
00:45:38,666 --> 00:45:40,567
- Robert Gallucci.
- Correct.

1098
00:45:40,568 --> 00:45:41,768
Yes!

1099
00:45:41,769 --> 00:45:43,837
That concludes all the questions.

1100
00:45:44,473 --> 00:45:47,540
How about Benjamin takes Baek Seok?

1101
00:45:47,541 --> 00:45:48,575
I'll do my best.

1102
00:45:48,576 --> 00:45:49,842
(Round 3: Baek Seok, Messenger: Benjamin)

1103
00:45:49,843 --> 00:45:50,710
Go!

1104
00:45:51,545 --> 00:45:52,745
They basically told me to do it.

1105
00:45:52,746 --> 00:45:54,081
They made the call for me.

1106
00:45:54,082 --> 00:45:56,348
"Your Korean isn't great,
so we won't really need you on the floor."

1107
00:45:56,349 --> 00:45:58,351
"Just go take Baek Seok."
That's what it felt like.

1108
00:46:01,055 --> 00:46:02,288
Yeou Nan Gol Jok.

1109
00:46:02,289 --> 00:46:03,657
("Yeou Nan Gol Jok,"
1935 poem by Baek Seok)

1110
00:46:03,658 --> 00:46:05,291
(Uses regional dialect to depict
a family gathering for the holidays)

1111
00:46:05,827 --> 00:46:09,095
Benjamin won't be able to pull this off.

1112
00:46:13,367 --> 00:46:14,367
Polar Bear.

1113
00:46:15,936 --> 00:46:17,203
Here's my dilemma.

1114
00:46:19,540 --> 00:46:22,842
What should I do about Superman?

1115
00:46:22,843 --> 00:46:25,879
I've actually built a pretty
decent rapport with him.

1116
00:46:25,880 --> 00:46:27,580
So have I.

1117
00:46:27,581 --> 00:46:29,849
You two clash quite a bit, don't you?

1118
00:46:29,850 --> 00:46:31,618
That honestly doesn't bother me.

1119
00:46:31,619 --> 00:46:33,520
I think we butt heads simply because...

1120
00:46:34,020 --> 00:46:36,422
we're both very outspoken.

1121
00:46:36,423 --> 00:46:39,491
The two of you are
already aligned, though.

1122
00:46:39,492 --> 00:46:42,661
If I step in, it might look
like we're forming a clique.

1123
00:46:42,662 --> 00:46:46,265
- Right.
- Let's not rush into anything.

1124
00:46:46,266 --> 00:46:48,334
As far as I'm concerned,

1125
00:46:48,335 --> 00:46:51,003
I see you as my leader.

1126
00:46:51,004 --> 00:46:54,707
- I appreciate that.
- Just know I have your back.

1127
00:46:54,708 --> 00:46:59,011
Today made me realize
just how addictive power can be.

1128
00:46:59,580 --> 00:47:04,506
Once again, the same people
naturally took charge,

1129
00:47:04,530 --> 00:47:06,376
almost like it was expected.

1130
00:47:06,787 --> 00:47:10,223
Having been
in that driver's seat myself...

1131
00:47:10,691 --> 00:47:12,257
I think we need to watch out for...

1132
00:47:12,726 --> 00:47:13,592
Dominating the room?

1133
00:47:13,593 --> 00:47:18,297
I think some people find that
pretty overbearing.

1134
00:47:18,298 --> 00:47:19,999
Because we come across as...

1135
00:47:20,000 --> 00:47:22,401
- Controlling too much.
- Exactly, so let's take a step back.

1136
00:47:22,402 --> 00:47:25,972
- Fair point.
- Even if things don't go our way,

1137
00:47:25,973 --> 00:47:27,873
I think we need to give ground
once or twice.

1138
00:47:27,874 --> 00:47:31,143
Because if we just argue logic,
we'll keep running the table.

1139
00:47:31,144 --> 00:47:32,812
- He's extremely competitive.
- Yeah.

1140
00:47:32,813 --> 00:47:34,447
- Very much so.
- He's got a lot of pride too.

1141
00:47:34,448 --> 00:47:35,348
Totally.

1142
00:47:35,783 --> 00:47:37,883
I was genuinely taken aback earlier.

1143
00:47:37,884 --> 00:47:39,885
I understand he's fiercely competitive,

1144
00:47:39,886 --> 00:47:41,888
but it felt like he was
just shutting me down.

1145
00:47:43,191 --> 00:47:46,725
Superman does tend
to come on a bit strong.

1146
00:47:46,726 --> 00:47:48,861
So whenever he drops a line
I can't let slide,

1147
00:47:48,862 --> 00:47:50,763
I end up pushing back.

1148
00:47:51,165 --> 00:47:53,266
Let's try not to jump in
right away, though.

1149
00:47:53,267 --> 00:47:56,469
Give someone else a chance
to push back first.

1150
00:47:56,470 --> 00:47:59,139
Just hang back and let that play out.

1151
00:47:59,140 --> 00:48:00,673
Got it. Understood.

1152
00:48:00,674 --> 00:48:03,042
Even if I don't speak up,
someone else will.

1153
00:48:03,043 --> 00:48:05,178
I don't want to look
like I'm trying to control everything.

1154
00:48:05,779 --> 00:48:07,813
The round three quiz begins now.

1155
00:48:07,814 --> 00:48:09,348
I had no idea what any of it meant.

1156
00:48:09,349 --> 00:48:12,685
The more I read it aloud, though,
the clearer the scene became.

1157
00:48:12,686 --> 00:48:14,120
Mom, Dad.

1158
00:48:14,121 --> 00:48:15,788
Three aunts.

1159
00:48:15,789 --> 00:48:17,590
One uncle.

1160
00:48:17,591 --> 00:48:20,092
It's about the extended family.

1161
00:48:20,327 --> 00:48:21,927
- Five siblings.
- Correct.

1162
00:48:21,928 --> 00:48:23,029
Yes!

1163
00:48:23,330 --> 00:48:25,131
- Shilli.
- Correct.

1164
00:48:26,700 --> 00:48:28,334
He plays a weaving game.

1165
00:48:28,335 --> 00:48:29,368
Incorrect.

1166
00:48:30,337 --> 00:48:31,771
This is brutal.

1167
00:48:32,206 --> 00:48:34,207
- The warmest spot by the hearth.
- Incorrect.

1168
00:48:34,208 --> 00:48:36,075
Ah, so close!

1169
00:48:36,410 --> 00:48:38,478
That concludes all the questions.

1170
00:48:38,479 --> 00:48:39,779
We still managed to get half right.

1171
00:48:39,780 --> 00:48:40,713
Yeah.

1172
00:48:40,714 --> 00:48:43,116
The round four quiz begins now.

1173
00:48:45,352 --> 00:48:46,952
(Round 4 topic: Latin)

1174
00:48:46,953 --> 00:48:48,387
Amor vincit.

1175
00:48:50,924 --> 00:48:53,158
Pondus. What is pondus?

1176
00:48:53,159 --> 00:48:56,128
This, too, shall pass.

1177
00:48:56,129 --> 00:48:57,429
Amor means love.

1178
00:48:57,430 --> 00:49:00,199
Vincit is like victory.

1179
00:49:00,200 --> 00:49:02,635
Omnia, like omniscience.

1180
00:49:02,636 --> 00:49:05,204
Amari, if you want to be loved, ama, love.

1181
00:49:05,205 --> 00:49:06,472
Si vis amari, ama.

1182
00:49:06,473 --> 00:49:09,074
I speak English, and so does Benjamin.

1183
00:49:09,075 --> 00:49:11,176
I also know some Spanish,

1184
00:49:11,177 --> 00:49:12,611
so a lot of the root words overlap.

1185
00:49:12,612 --> 00:49:15,047
I felt really confident about this round.

1186
00:49:15,282 --> 00:49:17,116
I drink, therefore I am.

1187
00:49:17,317 --> 00:49:18,317
Correct.

1188
00:49:18,318 --> 00:49:20,219
If you wish to be loved, love.

1189
00:49:20,220 --> 00:49:21,186
Correct.

1190
00:49:21,288 --> 00:49:23,155
It had something to do with words.

1191
00:49:23,156 --> 00:49:25,358
- Words...
- No.

1192
00:49:25,359 --> 00:49:27,193
Let's use a hint.

1193
00:49:27,194 --> 00:49:30,196
Five hundred dollars have been deducted
from the communal fund.

1194
00:49:30,864 --> 00:49:32,097
Words fade away.

1195
00:49:32,366 --> 00:49:33,266
Correct.

1196
00:49:34,067 --> 00:49:34,933
Tecum.

1197
00:49:35,602 --> 00:49:36,969
Where did that come from?

1198
00:49:37,571 --> 00:49:38,804
Hint, please.

1199
00:49:38,805 --> 00:49:41,807
Five hundred dollars have been deducted
from the communal fund.

1200
00:49:42,509 --> 00:49:43,375
Together.

1201
00:49:43,810 --> 00:49:44,910
Incorrect.

1202
00:49:46,646 --> 00:49:48,747
That concludes all the questions.

1203
00:49:49,182 --> 00:49:50,516
Great job, everyone.

1204
00:49:50,517 --> 00:49:51,484
Yeah.

1205
00:49:51,485 --> 00:49:52,785
That was exhausting.

1206
00:49:53,687 --> 00:49:55,421
- It was fun, though.
- Really fun.

1207
00:49:56,623 --> 00:49:57,923
That's what makes it so rewarding.

1208
00:49:57,924 --> 00:49:59,126
- Definitely.
- They're back.

1209
00:50:01,328 --> 00:50:03,396
(The mission team returns.)

1210
00:50:03,397 --> 00:50:05,264
You're back!

1211
00:50:05,265 --> 00:50:07,366
We've been waiting for you guys.

1212
00:50:07,367 --> 00:50:09,268
Welcome back!

1213
00:50:09,804 --> 00:50:10,769
Great work!

1214
00:50:11,171 --> 00:50:13,005
Come sit down first.

1215
00:50:13,006 --> 00:50:15,040
What took you guys so long?

1216
00:50:15,909 --> 00:50:17,110
We're completely drained.

1217
00:50:17,277 --> 00:50:18,510
I'm so curious.

1218
00:50:18,612 --> 00:50:20,513
Tell us everything!

1219
00:50:20,781 --> 00:50:23,216
We're probably all starving,
so let's order food first.

1220
00:50:23,217 --> 00:50:25,618
Good idea. What are we getting?

1221
00:50:25,619 --> 00:50:28,054
We'll grab some groceries and meal kits...

1222
00:50:28,055 --> 00:50:29,455
and cook for everyone.

1223
00:50:30,191 --> 00:50:31,724
Order placed.

1224
00:50:32,025 --> 00:50:33,525
The cost will be deducted
from the communal fund.

1225
00:50:33,660 --> 00:50:36,495
You will be notified
once the delivery arrives.

1226
00:50:40,200 --> 00:50:43,502
Today's mission team brought in...

1227
00:50:44,071 --> 00:50:46,939
a total of 12,500 dollars.

1228
00:50:46,981 --> 00:50:52,300
(Prize earned: 12,500 dollars,
Communal fund used: 1,000 dollars)

1229
00:50:54,114 --> 00:50:58,217
Here's how I think we should split it.

1230
00:50:58,452 --> 00:51:02,455
One thousand dollars each.

1231
00:51:02,556 --> 00:51:06,926
Since I already receive 1,000
from the communal fund,

1232
00:51:07,828 --> 00:51:12,198
I'll just take my cut from that.

1233
00:51:12,532 --> 00:51:17,836
The other 11 of you can take 1,000 each.

1234
00:51:17,837 --> 00:51:20,305
That leaves us with 1,500 dollars.

1235
00:51:20,608 --> 00:51:22,808
If we break that
into 500-dollar increments,

1236
00:51:23,076 --> 00:51:25,577
I'd like to award them as bonuses...

1237
00:51:25,578 --> 00:51:29,682
to the MVPs who really carried today.

1238
00:51:29,683 --> 00:51:31,016
That was my thought on the way back.

1239
00:51:31,017 --> 00:51:32,518
We absolutely should.

1240
00:51:32,519 --> 00:51:34,620
It belongs to the people
who put in the work.

1241
00:51:34,621 --> 00:51:36,422
I do have one question, though.

1242
00:51:36,423 --> 00:51:39,825
How do you decide
who contributed the most?

1243
00:51:39,826 --> 00:51:43,228
The ones who were there are actually
in total agreement on that.

1244
00:51:43,229 --> 00:51:44,329
Oh, really?

1245
00:51:44,330 --> 00:51:45,931
Yeah. There are three, right?

1246
00:51:46,233 --> 00:51:48,701
- Yes.
- And all three...

1247
00:51:49,202 --> 00:51:51,036
are the women on the team.

1248
00:51:51,305 --> 00:51:53,572
- Exactly what I thought.
- They carried the entire game.

1249
00:51:58,311 --> 00:52:00,045
Those three today...

1250
00:52:00,580 --> 00:52:03,081
were absolute masterminds
who carried the whole team.

1251
00:52:03,082 --> 00:52:04,049
Could not agree more.

1252
00:52:04,050 --> 00:52:07,019
(MVPs Sugar, Hippo, Genie:
500 dollars bonus each)

1253
00:52:07,154 --> 00:52:10,022
I got actual chills. No exaggeration.

1254
00:52:10,023 --> 00:52:11,324
I've never seen anything like it.

1255
00:52:11,325 --> 00:52:15,294
I felt like there was really no place
for me to contribute today.

1256
00:52:15,295 --> 00:52:18,131
It left me with a little sense
of alienation.

1257
00:52:18,298 --> 00:52:19,765
When you're part of a community,

1258
00:52:19,866 --> 00:52:22,201
you have to contribute...

1259
00:52:22,202 --> 00:52:26,171
to earn your place in it.

1260
00:52:26,172 --> 00:52:29,575
If you just hang around
without ever pulling your weight,

1261
00:52:29,576 --> 00:52:31,643
I feel like you eventually
get left behind.

1262
00:52:32,045 --> 00:52:35,848
I guess I did feel a bit small today.

1263
00:52:36,049 --> 00:52:38,217
We need to decide how much
to put into the fund...

1264
00:52:38,218 --> 00:52:41,186
and split the money up pretty quickly.

1265
00:52:41,488 --> 00:52:44,023
But there's one thing
I think we should consider.

1266
00:52:44,024 --> 00:52:46,392
What if we completely fail
the next activity?

1267
00:52:46,393 --> 00:52:49,762
If there's nothing left in the fund,
we can't even cover food.

1268
00:52:49,763 --> 00:52:51,764
- Or other basic needs.
- We also need to consider...

1269
00:52:51,765 --> 00:52:54,066
the possibility that we might not
even have enough money...

1270
00:52:54,067 --> 00:52:57,002
to try again the next day.

1271
00:52:57,003 --> 00:52:59,004
We need to keep some reserved...

1272
00:52:59,005 --> 00:53:01,774
for food or another attempt.

1273
00:53:01,775 --> 00:53:05,511
To be safe, I think we should
set aside enough for two days.

1274
00:53:05,512 --> 00:53:06,812
I agree.

1275
00:53:07,147 --> 00:53:08,247
We might come back with no money.

1276
00:53:08,248 --> 00:53:10,583
Right now, we're focused on...

1277
00:53:10,584 --> 00:53:13,752
how much of the communal fund
we can spend.

1278
00:53:13,753 --> 00:53:15,755
Instead, we could set a
2,000-dollar minimum...

1279
00:53:15,756 --> 00:53:16,989
for the communal fund.

1280
00:53:17,391 --> 00:53:18,857
It might be more reasonable
to set a minimum like that.

1281
00:53:18,858 --> 00:53:20,526
"We must have at least that much left
at the end of each day."

1282
00:53:20,527 --> 00:53:23,695
- Even if we get by on just ramyeon.
- That's a great idea.

1283
00:53:24,064 --> 00:53:26,732
Simply put, we're agreeing
on a minimum amount...

1284
00:53:26,733 --> 00:53:27,867
the community needs to get by.

1285
00:53:27,868 --> 00:53:30,503
How about we set the minimum at 2,000?

1286
00:53:30,971 --> 00:53:33,706
Shall we take a vote by a show of hands?

1287
00:53:34,140 --> 00:53:36,208
Who wants 20 percent?

1288
00:53:38,378 --> 00:53:39,979
- It's unanimous.
- I think it's already 30.

1289
00:53:39,980 --> 00:53:41,380
We'll take 30 percent then.

1290
00:53:41,381 --> 00:53:42,915
Alright then.

1291
00:53:43,717 --> 00:53:46,319
Your order has been delivered.

1292
00:53:47,721 --> 00:53:50,021
Yes! The food is here!

1293
00:53:50,156 --> 00:53:51,857
Let's make kimchi stew.

1294
00:53:51,858 --> 00:53:53,091
Yes. Let's do that.

1295
00:53:54,895 --> 00:53:57,229
We feel bad that it's
always you two cooking.

1296
00:53:57,230 --> 00:53:58,831
Is there anything I could help with?

1297
00:53:58,832 --> 00:53:59,765
If we can sort those out,

1298
00:53:59,766 --> 00:54:02,301
we could get an idea
of the remaining missions.

1299
00:54:02,302 --> 00:54:03,302
It's this one, Bruegel.

1300
00:54:03,303 --> 00:54:04,837
- We had Bruegel.
- Take this out.

1301
00:54:04,838 --> 00:54:05,938
We already took this one out too.

1302
00:54:05,939 --> 00:54:07,773
We need to talk about elimination too.

1303
00:54:07,774 --> 00:54:09,942
The ideology check is
really the only way...

1304
00:54:09,943 --> 00:54:12,244
anyone can be eliminated.

1305
00:54:12,245 --> 00:54:14,046
And Benjamin said earlier
that he wanted...

1306
00:54:14,047 --> 00:54:15,281
to talk about the ideology checks.

1307
00:54:15,282 --> 00:54:17,350
So I thought it'd be good
for the leaders...

1308
00:54:17,351 --> 00:54:19,352
to discuss it among themselves.

1309
00:54:19,353 --> 00:54:21,454
Today,

1310
00:54:21,922 --> 00:54:26,659
I'm going to use my ideology check...

1311
00:54:26,660 --> 00:54:28,427
on someone unlikely to be eliminated...

1312
00:54:28,428 --> 00:54:29,562
to get rid of mine.

1313
00:54:30,630 --> 00:54:33,065
We can't force people to do that.

1314
00:54:33,266 --> 00:54:36,604
But I think it'd be healthier
if we all did something along those lines.

1315
00:54:36,605 --> 00:54:38,771
You're right. Either use them early,

1316
00:54:38,772 --> 00:54:41,473
or agree to deliberately get them wrong.

1317
00:54:42,876 --> 00:54:47,179
The spy isn't here to eliminate anyone.

1318
00:54:47,180 --> 00:54:48,914
Once you're eliminated, it's over.

1319
00:54:48,915 --> 00:54:51,150
You have to survive first
before you can do anything.

1320
00:54:51,585 --> 00:54:54,253
"Don't die." That's my strategy.

1321
00:54:54,754 --> 00:54:58,757
I'll talk about it later,
but here's what I was thinking.

1322
00:54:58,992 --> 00:55:03,128
There's an uncomfortable truth...

1323
00:55:03,296 --> 00:55:05,964
that everyone is avoiding right now.

1324
00:55:06,399 --> 00:55:07,666
We have to attack.

1325
00:55:08,735 --> 00:55:10,837
We can't keep fooling ourselves...

1326
00:55:10,838 --> 00:55:12,237
under the name of "community."

1327
00:55:12,238 --> 00:55:13,505
It's just the truth.

1328
00:55:14,007 --> 00:55:17,209
The attack system was made for a reason.

1329
00:55:17,944 --> 00:55:21,546
There are two systems at play.
One is "The Community,"

1330
00:55:21,748 --> 00:55:24,116
and the other is the "ideology check."

1331
00:55:24,919 --> 00:55:28,321
I don't think they coexist.

1332
00:55:28,322 --> 00:55:31,390
I honestly don't see
any way they can coexist.

1333
00:55:31,558 --> 00:55:35,661
If you ask what could bring
everyone together,

1334
00:55:35,963 --> 00:55:37,295
it's finding a common enemy.

1335
00:55:38,432 --> 00:55:41,834
That's why I brought up the spy earlier.

1336
00:55:42,003 --> 00:55:43,569
Most people here right now are saying,

1337
00:55:43,737 --> 00:55:46,172
"Let's just put that risk..."

1338
00:55:46,173 --> 00:55:47,673
"aside for now." But one of them could...

1339
00:55:47,674 --> 00:55:49,041
- easily be...
- Exactly.

1340
00:55:49,042 --> 00:55:50,443
- the spy.
- That's right.

1341
00:55:50,444 --> 00:55:52,745
Honestly, if I were the spy,

1342
00:55:52,746 --> 00:55:54,313
this situation is...

1343
00:55:55,615 --> 00:55:57,015
the perfect setup.

1344
00:55:57,751 --> 00:55:59,885
I actually thought about this too.

1345
00:55:59,886 --> 00:56:01,887
If we all agree, we could pair up.

1346
00:56:01,888 --> 00:56:04,457
We could each reveal one
and deliberately get the other's wrong.

1347
00:56:04,458 --> 00:56:07,693
So basically, we pair people up 1-on-1.

1348
00:56:07,694 --> 00:56:08,995
Then no one has to risk being eliminated.

1349
00:56:08,996 --> 00:56:10,596
Only the two will know.

1350
00:56:11,164 --> 00:56:12,698
Then the spy wouldn't know either.

1351
00:56:13,166 --> 00:56:14,466
I think this is the safest.

1352
00:56:14,467 --> 00:56:16,235
Revealing it to everyone
would be too risky.

1353
00:56:16,236 --> 00:56:18,370
So just pair up with someone you trust,

1354
00:56:18,371 --> 00:56:20,205
and say, "Let's get rid of ours together."

1355
00:56:20,206 --> 00:56:21,406
Then you'd know.

1356
00:56:21,407 --> 00:56:24,777
- That's so smart.
- And the spy can't do anything.

1357
00:56:24,778 --> 00:56:26,078
If someone dies...

1358
00:56:26,079 --> 00:56:26,879
Then that's the spy at work.

1359
00:56:26,880 --> 00:56:30,115
Plus, one of the former leaders said
they'd save us.

1360
00:56:30,116 --> 00:56:31,550
They can use immunity,

1361
00:56:31,651 --> 00:56:32,918
expose the spy,

1362
00:56:32,919 --> 00:56:33,752
but also survive.

1363
00:56:33,753 --> 00:56:34,519
(Using a leader's immunity)

1364
00:56:35,889 --> 00:56:38,224
So if we were to pair up
and the spy kills one of us,

1365
00:56:38,225 --> 00:56:40,092
we can save each other
and know who did it.

1366
00:56:40,093 --> 00:56:42,862
So revealing the pairings might
actually keep the spy in check.

1367
00:56:43,396 --> 00:56:44,796
They wouldn't be able to make
a move easily.

1368
00:56:45,632 --> 00:56:46,799
Because they'll be exposed.

1369
00:56:47,233 --> 00:56:50,035
We're so sorry we keep getting
in your way, Mr. Spy.

1370
00:56:50,036 --> 00:56:51,770
- Sorry.
- But we can't help it.

1371
00:56:52,038 --> 00:56:56,775
(The Community Center)

1372
00:56:57,143 --> 00:56:59,478
I came here to get paid,
but I have to figure out how to split it.

1373
00:57:01,681 --> 00:57:05,417
(Giving bonuses to Genie, Sugar,
and Hippo)

1374
00:57:05,418 --> 00:57:07,118
(Residents: 1,000 dollars)

1375
00:57:07,119 --> 00:57:08,753
(Genie, Sugar, Hippo: 1,500 dollars,
Benjamin: 0 dollars)

1376
00:57:09,789 --> 00:57:14,092
(Money allocation complete)

1377
00:57:14,327 --> 00:57:15,761
(30 percent collected
for the communal fund)

1378
00:57:15,762 --> 00:57:17,296
Thirty percent.

1379
00:57:18,531 --> 00:57:21,166
(Additional money added)

1380
00:57:21,167 --> 00:57:24,202
(Individual Fund Distribution)

1381
00:57:24,203 --> 00:57:27,639
(30 percent collected
for the communal fund)

1382
00:57:27,640 --> 00:57:30,075
(Leader's personal fund: 1,000 dollars)

1383
00:57:30,076 --> 00:57:31,676
(Benjamin moves to second place.)

1384
00:57:33,580 --> 00:57:35,814
Once per day,

1385
00:57:36,316 --> 00:57:39,784
the spy may view
one resident's full ideology code.

1386
00:57:40,253 --> 00:57:42,521
Please choose the resident
you would like to view.

1387
00:57:45,558 --> 00:57:46,725
Okay.

1388
00:57:47,961 --> 00:57:49,528
One person I'd like to know.

1389
00:57:50,964 --> 00:57:52,831
(Sighing)

1390
00:57:53,700 --> 00:57:54,800
Who should I kill?

1391
00:57:59,339 --> 00:58:00,472
I think it's Benjamin.

1392
00:58:01,241 --> 00:58:06,712
(Polar Bear may be onto his identity.)

1393
00:58:07,046 --> 00:58:09,947
To be honest, when I was talking about...

1394
00:58:09,948 --> 00:58:16,855
(Michael forces him to show his hand.)

1395
00:58:17,056 --> 00:58:18,056
A royal inspector's badge!

1396
00:58:18,057 --> 00:58:23,028
(Reporter Nangja now has access
to information.)

1397
00:58:29,803 --> 00:58:30,803
Thank you.

1398
00:58:33,940 --> 00:58:35,908
- Thank you for the food!
- It looks so good.

1399
00:58:35,909 --> 00:58:37,342
Enjoy your meal.

1400
00:58:37,343 --> 00:58:38,577
It's basically half broth, half meat.

1401
00:58:38,578 --> 00:58:40,479
I think that's the best part.

1402
00:58:47,754 --> 00:58:48,787
It's so good.

1403
00:58:49,689 --> 00:58:52,391
If you enjoyed the food,
please give me your favorability votes.

1404
00:58:52,725 --> 00:58:55,060
- Of course.
- Thank you!

1405
00:58:55,061 --> 00:58:57,194
I will be checking tomorrow morning.

1406
00:58:57,195 --> 00:58:58,196
It's really good.

1407
00:58:58,498 --> 00:59:00,899
It's insane.

1408
00:59:02,769 --> 00:59:04,070
This tastes amazing.

1409
00:59:04,071 --> 00:59:06,439
We don't know when that's going to pop up,

1410
00:59:06,440 --> 00:59:09,074
so can I explain it while we eat?

1411
00:59:09,309 --> 00:59:10,409
The matching thing.

1412
00:59:10,410 --> 00:59:12,344
- Yes.
- We're not forcing anything.

1413
00:59:12,345 --> 00:59:15,214
It's only for those who want to.

1414
00:59:15,748 --> 00:59:17,682
If you have an attack token...

1415
00:59:17,683 --> 00:59:20,218
and don't want to hold onto it...

1416
00:59:20,219 --> 00:59:22,454
until the end,
meaning you lose 5,000 dollars.

1417
00:59:22,455 --> 00:59:25,056
Or for the people...

1418
00:59:25,057 --> 00:59:26,391
who'd rather keep things peaceful,

1419
00:59:26,693 --> 00:59:29,194
if you use it today, even if you fail,

1420
00:59:29,195 --> 00:59:30,729
you only lose 2,000 dollars.

1421
00:59:30,930 --> 00:59:32,964
So through a 1-on-1 pairing...

1422
00:59:32,965 --> 00:59:35,033
with someone you trust,

1423
00:59:35,368 --> 00:59:37,369
you only need to reveal...

1424
00:59:37,370 --> 00:59:38,904
one letter from your code.

1425
00:59:38,905 --> 00:59:39,971
You don't need to give any numbers.

1426
00:59:39,972 --> 00:59:41,907
Get that one answer wrong on purpose...

1427
00:59:41,908 --> 00:59:43,809
and put any numbers you want for the rest.

1428
00:59:43,810 --> 00:59:44,943
There's no way you'll get it all right.

1429
00:59:44,944 --> 00:59:47,646
So take turns doing an ideology check...

1430
00:59:47,647 --> 00:59:48,680
with your partner.

1431
00:59:48,681 --> 00:59:52,351
Then go back to your rooms and check
whether you got an attacker alert.

1432
00:59:52,352 --> 00:59:54,453
That'll complete the process.

1433
00:59:54,787 --> 00:59:56,421
Of course, you don't have to do this...

1434
00:59:56,422 --> 00:59:57,322
if you don't want to.

1435
00:59:57,323 --> 00:59:58,656
When did that rule come up?

1436
00:59:58,657 --> 01:00:00,992
- You can just pair up with whom you want.
- When did that come out?

1437
01:00:00,993 --> 01:00:03,762
There's no way this was brought up
while we were smoking.

1438
01:00:03,763 --> 01:00:06,097
I did hear a little about this earlier,

1439
01:00:06,098 --> 01:00:09,234
about the whole 1-on-1 pairing.

1440
01:00:09,235 --> 01:00:11,269
This is just one of the many ideas.

1441
01:00:13,172 --> 01:00:15,240
So we just have to do this today?

1442
01:00:17,076 --> 01:00:19,477
I think we should all do it.

1443
01:00:20,613 --> 01:00:23,248
Ted.

1444
01:00:23,583 --> 01:00:24,650
Did you choose your pair?

1445
01:00:25,051 --> 01:00:27,085
No, I'm still open. I'd like to pair up.

1446
01:00:27,086 --> 01:00:28,253
Do you want to pair up? Okay.

1447
01:00:28,755 --> 01:00:30,155
You just need to pair up like this.

1448
01:00:37,797 --> 01:00:39,498
I might be overthinking.

1449
01:00:40,500 --> 01:00:43,302
How do I know if this is all a gimmick?

1450
01:00:43,503 --> 01:00:45,370
It's too perfect.

1451
01:00:45,672 --> 01:00:47,906
- Too perfect.
- You're right.

1452
01:00:47,907 --> 01:00:50,676
The pair-up idea is also very obvious.

1453
01:00:50,977 --> 01:00:53,312
Pairing up with people like it's nothing?

1454
01:00:53,913 --> 01:00:55,280
That's unnatural.

1455
01:00:55,281 --> 01:00:56,081
Right.

1456
01:00:56,816 --> 01:01:00,419
It's as if it was already scripted.

1457
01:01:01,688 --> 01:01:03,288
- True.
- It is.

1458
01:01:04,424 --> 01:01:05,324
Yes.

1459
01:01:12,065 --> 01:01:13,632
- Are you going to do it?
- Yes.

1460
01:01:14,500 --> 01:01:15,934
With Polar Bear.

1461
01:01:16,336 --> 01:01:20,072
(Gray and Polar Bear were
also on the income-earning team on Day 2.)

1462
01:01:20,306 --> 01:01:23,275
I'm curious as to why you're all doing it
with the people who stayed.

1463
01:01:23,276 --> 01:01:24,709
- The pairing?
- Yes.

1464
01:01:25,078 --> 01:01:26,411
I see.

1465
01:01:26,546 --> 01:01:29,048
- It feels a little suspicious to me too.
- Then let's mix it up.

1466
01:01:29,049 --> 01:01:32,584
No, because we heard this afterward.
We're wondering if you all talked.

1467
01:01:32,752 --> 01:01:33,953
We didn't.

1468
01:01:33,954 --> 01:01:37,322
It was only brought up
after Sugar and Hippo came.

1469
01:01:37,323 --> 01:01:39,957
Maybe I'm reading too much into it,

1470
01:01:39,958 --> 01:01:43,929
but what's funny is the pairs
just naturally ended up being...

1471
01:01:43,930 --> 01:01:45,464
Superman and Ted.

1472
01:01:45,665 --> 01:01:46,964
Gray and Polar Bear.

1473
01:01:46,965 --> 01:01:49,434
- Then, would you like to do it with me?
- You can do it with me.

1474
01:01:49,435 --> 01:01:51,803
If you want to pair up, I can change.

1475
01:01:51,804 --> 01:01:52,904
Me too.

1476
01:01:53,306 --> 01:01:54,640
- Am I being too sensitive?
- Huh?

1477
01:01:54,641 --> 01:01:55,674
Am I being too sensitive?

1478
01:01:56,142 --> 01:01:59,111
No, I think it's a valid suspicion.

1479
01:02:01,147 --> 01:02:03,081
I was already ignored...

1480
01:02:03,082 --> 01:02:06,284
by Superman this morning.

1481
01:02:06,519 --> 01:02:08,353
Then he suddenly raises his hand.

1482
01:02:08,354 --> 01:02:10,255
"Who wants to pair up with me?"

1483
01:02:10,789 --> 01:02:12,490
That's what shocked me.

1484
01:02:12,658 --> 01:02:14,592
They already made the rules...

1485
01:02:14,994 --> 01:02:16,361
when I was away.

1486
01:02:16,528 --> 01:02:19,197
They never discussed it...

1487
01:02:19,198 --> 01:02:21,800
when they said that their priority is
the community.

1488
01:02:21,801 --> 01:02:25,271
I thought, "They're excluding me
this blatantly?"

1489
01:02:25,272 --> 01:02:27,439
There's zero respect for me here.

1490
01:02:27,440 --> 01:02:30,242
If they considered me as one of them,
they wouldn't be doing this.

1491
01:02:30,243 --> 01:02:31,910
I can't take this.

1492
01:02:31,911 --> 01:02:35,179
I'm 100 percent sure there's a spy.

1493
01:02:35,180 --> 01:02:37,182
But this is the perfect chance...

1494
01:02:37,183 --> 01:02:39,350
for the spy to kill someone.

1495
01:02:39,351 --> 01:02:42,053
I'd like to wait things out for today.

1496
01:02:42,054 --> 01:02:43,788
Personally.

1497
01:02:44,223 --> 01:02:48,092
What I want to avoid most is...

1498
01:02:48,261 --> 01:02:51,129
someone getting eliminated...

1499
01:02:51,130 --> 01:02:53,999
for no real reason.

1500
01:02:54,000 --> 01:02:55,466
True.

1501
01:02:55,467 --> 01:02:57,603
For no reason.

1502
01:02:57,604 --> 01:02:58,805
Hold on.

1503
01:02:58,806 --> 01:03:00,706
I'd like to say one thing.

1504
01:03:00,707 --> 01:03:01,540
- Okay.
- So,

1505
01:03:01,541 --> 01:03:03,875
if one person is taken out,

1506
01:03:04,077 --> 01:03:06,412
it creates a flaw
in the system we've set up.

1507
01:03:06,712 --> 01:03:08,080
Say this person is the spy...

1508
01:03:08,081 --> 01:03:09,380
and this person gets attacked.

1509
01:03:09,549 --> 01:03:11,149
Then there are two possible suspects.

1510
01:03:11,150 --> 01:03:12,150
(You can't be sure who attacked.)

1511
01:03:12,151 --> 01:03:13,719
Instead of having
just one person to suspect,

1512
01:03:13,720 --> 01:03:15,354
- now we have two.
- So yes,

1513
01:03:15,355 --> 01:03:17,122
the system will start to crack a little.

1514
01:03:17,123 --> 01:03:18,824
Let's say I pair up with someone.

1515
01:03:19,092 --> 01:03:21,627
If I'm really unlucky,

1516
01:03:21,994 --> 01:03:25,396
I could still end up getting eliminated.

1517
01:03:25,397 --> 01:03:26,264
Then you'll be revived.

1518
01:03:26,265 --> 01:03:28,366
We promised to revive the person.

1519
01:03:28,367 --> 01:03:29,767
But can you guarantee...

1520
01:03:30,470 --> 01:03:32,237
that'll happen?

1521
01:03:32,238 --> 01:03:34,740
We've had three leaders so far, right?

1522
01:03:34,741 --> 01:03:36,008
- Yes.
- There are three immunity tokens.

1523
01:03:36,009 --> 01:03:39,178
Assuming all the leaders agree to use
theirs to save whoever gets eliminated,

1524
01:03:39,179 --> 01:03:39,978
Right.

1525
01:03:39,979 --> 01:03:42,014
then the spy is guaranteed to fail today.

1526
01:03:42,015 --> 01:03:43,782
Even if the spy attacks, they'll survive.

1527
01:03:44,016 --> 01:03:46,317
This can let us survive three more days.

1528
01:03:46,318 --> 01:03:47,286
Right.

1529
01:03:47,287 --> 01:03:49,120
I think it'll let us survive
until at least Day Six.

1530
01:03:49,121 --> 01:03:50,555
Today is already Day Three.

1531
01:03:51,224 --> 01:03:52,090
So at least until Day Six.

1532
01:03:52,091 --> 01:03:54,026
There is an additional announcement.

1533
01:03:55,929 --> 01:03:57,596
Today, the spy viewed...

1534
01:03:57,930 --> 01:04:01,199
one resident's full ideology code.

1535
01:04:04,770 --> 01:04:07,105
- Hold on, let's focus.
- This is so important.

1536
01:04:07,106 --> 01:04:09,207
- I got chills.
- That concludes the announcement.

1537
01:04:12,078 --> 01:04:14,012
- The spy viewed one?
- One of the residents.

1538
01:04:14,213 --> 01:04:15,547
I got goosebumps.

1539
01:04:16,782 --> 01:04:17,849
Goodness.

1540
01:04:18,150 --> 01:04:20,218
So the spy knows someone's exact code?

1541
01:04:20,219 --> 01:04:22,821
Yes. So, we have to pair up.

1542
01:04:23,289 --> 01:04:24,756
So there really is a spy?

1543
01:04:25,158 --> 01:04:27,826
Of course there is.

1544
01:04:28,261 --> 01:04:29,394
This is a mess.

1545
01:04:30,096 --> 01:04:33,665
So this settles the topic we were
discussing with the Reporter.

1546
01:04:33,666 --> 01:04:35,834
We now know for sure that there is a spy.

1547
01:04:35,835 --> 01:04:37,702
That's right.

1548
01:04:37,937 --> 01:04:38,970
Yes.

1549
01:04:39,205 --> 01:04:41,873
It's finally been proved, you fools!

1550
01:04:42,375 --> 01:04:44,042
This is a survival.

1551
01:04:44,043 --> 01:04:46,311
Feeling a little more cautious now?

1552
01:04:46,312 --> 01:04:48,547
That cleared up a lot of my frustration.

1553
01:04:48,816 --> 01:04:50,615
Is it about to start now?

1554
01:04:50,850 --> 01:04:52,551
Then what do I need to do
to prepare myself?

1555
01:04:53,085 --> 01:04:55,219
To be honest,

1556
01:04:55,220 --> 01:04:57,889
I wasn't entirely cautious.

1557
01:04:57,890 --> 01:05:01,626
But after learning that the spy viewed
someone's code,

1558
01:05:01,627 --> 01:05:04,162
finding the spy became top priority.

1559
01:05:06,232 --> 01:05:08,066
Now we must pair up.

1560
01:05:08,067 --> 01:05:12,070
Then I think Ted's idea is the best way.

1561
01:05:12,404 --> 01:05:13,972
Yes. Since it's come to this,

1562
01:05:13,973 --> 01:05:15,006
we have to use this method.

1563
01:05:15,007 --> 01:05:17,241
After hearing that,

1564
01:05:17,242 --> 01:05:19,310
This is why I don't
feel like participating.

1565
01:05:19,311 --> 01:05:21,379
You just told us the solution.

1566
01:05:21,380 --> 01:05:23,415
- Using immunity.
- Right.

1567
01:05:23,416 --> 01:05:26,651
And the number of days checks out.

1568
01:05:26,652 --> 01:05:29,587
So my question is...

1569
01:05:29,588 --> 01:05:31,089
do we all have to attack?

1570
01:05:31,090 --> 01:05:33,025
That's a valid point.

1571
01:05:33,026 --> 01:05:35,560
I'd like to point out
that this isn't forced.

1572
01:05:35,561 --> 01:05:37,962
I have one question, though.

1573
01:05:37,963 --> 01:05:40,131
And first, I want to make it clear...

1574
01:05:40,132 --> 01:05:42,266
that I'm not trying to attack Michael.

1575
01:05:42,267 --> 01:05:45,870
We're setting up this system
so that no one gets eliminated.

1576
01:05:46,272 --> 01:05:47,807
And I think we're using our immunity...

1577
01:05:47,808 --> 01:05:49,975
because that's what we all agreed on.

1578
01:05:49,976 --> 01:05:52,344
But if someone acts
outside that agreement,

1579
01:05:52,345 --> 01:05:55,146
we need to discuss whether we should
still use it to save them.

1580
01:05:55,448 --> 01:05:57,082
I don't think I can agree to that.

1581
01:05:57,083 --> 01:05:59,484
I think that's a valid point as well.

1582
01:05:59,485 --> 01:06:00,685
I understand what you're saying.

1583
01:06:00,686 --> 01:06:02,120
I just wanted to bring that up.

1584
01:06:02,121 --> 01:06:03,622
That's way too...

1585
01:06:03,623 --> 01:06:04,790
You can go first, Nangja.

1586
01:06:04,791 --> 01:06:06,925
I think Polar Bear and I have
the same question.

1587
01:06:06,926 --> 01:06:08,860
You're choosing to stay
outside the system...

1588
01:06:08,861 --> 01:06:10,695
and keep acting on your own.

1589
01:06:10,696 --> 01:06:12,831
But if you end up getting eliminated,

1590
01:06:12,832 --> 01:06:14,366
you still expect us to save you, right?

1591
01:06:14,367 --> 01:06:15,200
Of course.

1592
01:06:15,201 --> 01:06:16,701
Even if you're not part of the system.

1593
01:06:17,336 --> 01:06:18,703
- Right.
- That's selfish.

1594
01:06:18,704 --> 01:06:21,139
I do want to push back on the idea...

1595
01:06:21,140 --> 01:06:22,774
that this is selfish.

1596
01:06:23,042 --> 01:06:25,077
- By all means.
- After I spoke up,

1597
01:06:25,078 --> 01:06:27,011
we confirmed that there is a spy...

1598
01:06:27,012 --> 01:06:28,513
and that they can access our codes.

1599
01:06:28,881 --> 01:06:30,649
That's a risk I'm taking.

1600
01:06:30,650 --> 01:06:34,752
Because now I'm more likely
to be targeted by the spy.

1601
01:06:34,954 --> 01:06:36,588
So I think it's a reasonable...

1602
01:06:37,089 --> 01:06:39,090
exchange. It's give and take.

1603
01:06:39,091 --> 01:06:42,827
Then, let me ask you directly, Michael.

1604
01:06:43,363 --> 01:06:46,565
What's the main reason...

1605
01:06:46,566 --> 01:06:48,166
you decided not to join this system?

1606
01:06:48,400 --> 01:06:50,068
Here's my concern.

1607
01:06:50,303 --> 01:06:52,971
If I join that system,

1608
01:06:53,072 --> 01:06:56,274
the spy could deliberately kill me...

1609
01:06:56,742 --> 01:06:58,409
- Target you.
- Yes.

1610
01:06:58,911 --> 01:07:00,445
and make it look like an accident.

1611
01:07:00,446 --> 01:07:04,816
They'll just make it look
like I got unlucky.

1612
01:07:04,817 --> 01:07:07,385
There's that possibility.

1613
01:07:07,820 --> 01:07:11,221
I'm opting out because I don't want...

1614
01:07:11,222 --> 01:07:12,357
to take that risk.

1615
01:07:12,358 --> 01:07:15,026
But that risk exists...

1616
01:07:15,027 --> 01:07:18,329
whether you're part of the system or not.

1617
01:07:18,330 --> 01:07:21,232
- You could get eliminated outside...
- It's the same whether you're in or out.

1618
01:07:21,233 --> 01:07:24,002
or in here by accident. It's the same.

1619
01:07:24,003 --> 01:07:27,172
When you explained the rules earlier,

1620
01:07:27,173 --> 01:07:28,940
you said anyone who wanted to join could,

1621
01:07:28,941 --> 01:07:31,109
and anyone who didn't
want to could opt out.

1622
01:07:31,110 --> 01:07:33,111
So I chose to opt out.

1623
01:07:33,112 --> 01:07:35,513
- We respect that.
- We respect that choice.

1624
01:07:35,514 --> 01:07:37,282
Your logic was just confusing.

1625
01:07:37,283 --> 01:07:38,950
Your opinion is still respected.

1626
01:07:39,285 --> 01:07:42,554
I couldn't understand...

1627
01:07:42,555 --> 01:07:44,122
why Michael wanted to opt out.

1628
01:07:44,123 --> 01:07:47,659
That made me wonder if he was the spy.

1629
01:07:47,993 --> 01:07:51,762
At that point, I was sure he was the spy.

1630
01:07:51,763 --> 01:07:54,332
And I realized he tends to push back...

1631
01:07:54,333 --> 01:07:55,933
whenever the group agrees on something.

1632
01:07:56,769 --> 01:07:59,637
Come on, Michael. Let's go for a smoke.

1633
01:07:59,638 --> 01:08:01,406
- Do it with me, Michael.
- Let's go for a smoke.

1634
01:08:01,407 --> 01:08:03,275
Do it with me.

1635
01:08:03,276 --> 01:08:05,544
- Are you going to do it or not?
- Not today.

1636
01:08:05,545 --> 01:08:07,078
Because...

1637
01:08:07,079 --> 01:08:09,680
I don't feel respected at all.

1638
01:08:09,681 --> 01:08:13,385
At first, they said,
"You're free to choose."

1639
01:08:13,386 --> 01:08:16,354
"You're free to opt out."

1640
01:08:16,355 --> 01:08:18,223
And now we know there's a spy.

1641
01:08:18,224 --> 01:08:19,991
I'm obviously going to be...

1642
01:08:19,992 --> 01:08:21,426
a target in this situation.

1643
01:08:21,427 --> 01:08:22,794
But they're going to save you.

1644
01:08:22,795 --> 01:08:24,362
I can't trust that yet.

1645
01:08:24,363 --> 01:08:26,097
- Really?
- Not 100 percent.

1646
01:08:26,098 --> 01:08:28,700
How long has it been
since they told us about the spy?

1647
01:08:28,701 --> 01:08:30,101
It's been less than 30 minutes.

1648
01:08:30,102 --> 01:08:32,237
I think it's way too rushed.

1649
01:08:32,238 --> 01:08:35,140
If that's what you think,
I'll pair up with someone else.

1650
01:08:35,141 --> 01:08:36,541
- Okay.
- Yes.

1651
01:08:36,542 --> 01:08:38,143
Alright, Michael. I get your point.

1652
01:08:38,410 --> 01:08:41,645
- I'm really sorry.
- Don't be!

1653
01:08:41,646 --> 01:08:43,013
- I'm sorry.
- I got your back.

1654
01:08:43,014 --> 01:08:45,015
Michael, you're free to opt out.

1655
01:08:45,016 --> 01:08:47,084
You don't have to do it
if you think it's too forced.

1656
01:08:47,085 --> 01:08:48,520
Yes.

1657
01:08:48,521 --> 01:08:50,688
I can see how this could feel
a bit communist.

1658
01:08:50,689 --> 01:08:53,958
I just feel like it's too rushed.

1659
01:08:53,959 --> 01:08:55,426
I understand that.

1660
01:08:55,427 --> 01:08:57,462
My opinion was ignored this morning.

1661
01:08:57,463 --> 01:09:00,731
And Superman is the one who ignored me.

1662
01:09:01,367 --> 01:09:02,901
I wasn't happy about that.

1663
01:09:02,902 --> 01:09:05,136
That's why I was a bit vocal about it.

1664
01:09:05,137 --> 01:09:07,138
I'm a member of this community too.

1665
01:09:07,939 --> 01:09:11,342
Why is asking about the spy...

1666
01:09:11,911 --> 01:09:13,345
completely off the table for the Reporter?

1667
01:09:13,346 --> 01:09:15,080
Why is that not
for the good of the community?

1668
01:09:15,614 --> 01:09:17,916
I'm just personally suspicious.

1669
01:09:17,917 --> 01:09:19,884
I'll be honest. Now we know there's a spy.

1670
01:09:19,885 --> 01:09:22,153
I suspect Superman.

1671
01:09:22,455 --> 01:09:25,824
And that very person I'm suspecting is...

1672
01:09:26,158 --> 01:09:29,827
calling me selfish...

1673
01:09:29,828 --> 01:09:31,496
for not joining the system.

1674
01:09:31,497 --> 01:09:33,364
Of course it feels...

1675
01:09:34,166 --> 01:09:35,900
forced for me.

1676
01:09:35,901 --> 01:09:38,436
If he is the spy,

1677
01:09:38,938 --> 01:09:40,906
I could totally see him doing this.

1678
01:09:40,907 --> 01:09:43,475
- Right.
- A spy would do exactly this.

1679
01:09:44,043 --> 01:09:48,045
The ideology check station is now open.

1680
01:09:48,581 --> 01:09:50,582
Residents who wish to conduct
an ideology check...

1681
01:09:50,950 --> 01:09:52,751
should visit the Community Center...

1682
01:09:52,752 --> 01:09:57,556
and say, "I would like to conduct
an ideology check."

1683
01:10:04,964 --> 01:10:08,967
(Michael is conflicted.)

1684
01:10:12,004 --> 01:10:14,138
So we just need to do this...

1685
01:10:14,139 --> 01:10:15,940
one by one, right?

1686
01:10:15,941 --> 01:10:18,009
We're doing it today, right?
Let's just go for it.

1687
01:10:18,844 --> 01:10:20,678
Aren't we still deciding on it?

1688
01:10:20,679 --> 01:10:22,079
Really?

1689
01:10:22,080 --> 01:10:23,581
We need Michael here.

1690
01:10:23,883 --> 01:10:25,951
We need him here too.

1691
01:10:25,952 --> 01:10:27,385
But some have already shared.

1692
01:10:27,386 --> 01:10:30,622
They can do it, but we need to ask
what Michael is going to do.

1693
01:10:31,991 --> 01:10:33,258
Who has already shared their info?

1694
01:10:34,661 --> 01:10:36,594
- Most of us did.
- Who's left?

1695
01:10:37,029 --> 01:10:39,564
You, me,

1696
01:10:40,432 --> 01:10:42,700
Dark Knight,

1697
01:10:42,701 --> 01:10:43,969
and Ted. Everyone else did it but us.

1698
01:10:43,970 --> 01:10:44,969
- Who?
- Ted.

1699
01:10:45,604 --> 01:10:48,005
- Ted.
- Ted and I were originally partners,

1700
01:10:48,006 --> 01:10:50,107
but you said no, so Dark Knight...

1701
01:10:50,108 --> 01:10:51,108
What are you going to do?

1702
01:10:51,109 --> 01:10:53,377
I'll tell you when everyone's back.

1703
01:10:54,332 --> 01:10:56,714
You can't keep dragging it like this.

1704
01:10:56,715 --> 01:10:57,882
Just tell us.

1705
01:11:03,255 --> 01:11:05,489
That's when it hit me.

1706
01:11:05,490 --> 01:11:07,124
I'd become the weird one.

1707
01:11:07,293 --> 01:11:09,093
The extremely weird one.

1708
01:11:09,428 --> 01:11:12,263
I went off spouting conspiracy theories.

1709
01:11:12,798 --> 01:11:13,965
I ruined the whole vibe.

1710
01:11:15,434 --> 01:11:17,268
I was close to being traumatized.

1711
01:11:17,269 --> 01:11:19,437
"Wow, I need to lay low."

1712
01:11:19,705 --> 01:11:21,439
"I should keep my mouth shut."

1713
01:11:21,807 --> 01:11:25,643
Here's what I have to say.

1714
01:11:25,978 --> 01:11:27,578
Earlier,

1715
01:11:27,579 --> 01:11:28,947
it felt very forced.

1716
01:11:28,948 --> 01:11:30,815
- Yes.
- It felt rushed.

1717
01:11:31,417 --> 01:11:35,220
Also, I felt very left out this morning.

1718
01:11:35,354 --> 01:11:36,888
I'll just cut to the chase.

1719
01:11:37,356 --> 01:11:38,756
If I keep acting like this...

1720
01:11:38,958 --> 01:11:41,293
in the community,

1721
01:11:41,294 --> 01:11:43,429
it really doesn't match the purpose.

1722
01:11:43,430 --> 01:11:44,729
So this time,

1723
01:11:45,931 --> 01:11:48,566
I've decided to participate.

1724
01:11:48,834 --> 01:11:50,568
And as for guaranteeing...

1725
01:11:50,569 --> 01:11:52,236
that anyone who gets eliminated...

1726
01:11:52,605 --> 01:11:54,072
will be saved,

1727
01:11:54,640 --> 01:11:56,374
I just want to make sure...

1728
01:11:56,375 --> 01:11:58,610
we've officially agreed on that as a rule.

1729
01:11:58,611 --> 01:11:59,911
It's been agreed upon.

1730
01:11:59,912 --> 01:12:02,814
It's guaranteed if you join
this agreement.

1731
01:12:02,815 --> 01:12:03,715
Got it.

1732
01:12:03,716 --> 01:12:05,883
Look at you. You're there all by yourself.

1733
01:12:05,884 --> 01:12:07,185
- Get in here.
- Come on.

1734
01:12:07,186 --> 01:12:08,886
Black and white.

1735
01:12:09,488 --> 01:12:11,189
- Black and white!
- So,

1736
01:12:11,557 --> 01:12:15,093
I'd like to pair up with Ted today.

1737
01:12:16,095 --> 01:12:19,064
To win these people's trust again,

1738
01:12:19,264 --> 01:12:22,967
I knew I had to join.

1739
01:12:22,968 --> 01:12:25,569
So I decided to join them.

1740
01:12:25,570 --> 01:12:27,104
I think it's only natural.

1741
01:12:27,105 --> 01:12:30,608
Everyone here is bound
by the community's norms.

1742
01:12:30,609 --> 01:12:34,078
That's what norms do.
They keep people in line.

1743
01:12:34,079 --> 01:12:36,046
It's hard to break out.

1744
01:12:36,047 --> 01:12:38,683
Even if someone disagrees,

1745
01:12:38,684 --> 01:12:39,984
they don't dare question it.

1746
01:12:40,319 --> 01:12:42,854
That's what makes social norms so scary.

1747
01:12:43,255 --> 01:12:44,689
They're powerful.

1748
01:12:46,692 --> 01:12:48,760
We only have 40 minutes to vote.

1749
01:12:48,761 --> 01:12:49,927
So let's hurry.

1750
01:12:50,329 --> 01:12:52,897
Let's gather and talk
once we're done voting.

1751
01:12:53,599 --> 01:12:55,800
Superman, you're with me.

1752
01:12:56,735 --> 01:12:57,969
We're partners.

1753
01:12:57,970 --> 01:12:59,637
Yes, the first righty.

1754
01:12:59,873 --> 01:13:01,338
The first righty?

1755
01:13:01,507 --> 01:13:02,474
Indeed.

1756
01:13:02,475 --> 01:13:03,575
I need to tell you too.

1757
01:13:03,576 --> 01:13:05,143
What is it?

1758
01:13:05,544 --> 01:13:06,410
Okay.

1759
01:13:06,411 --> 01:13:07,712
(They are sharing their codes.)

1760
01:13:07,780 --> 01:13:10,348
You're not the spy, right? I'm scared.

1761
01:13:11,150 --> 01:13:12,050
We need to swear on it.

1762
01:13:12,051 --> 01:13:13,084
(Go Ae Shin and Benjamin)

1763
01:13:13,085 --> 01:13:14,352
W.

1764
01:13:14,353 --> 01:13:15,888
Right, C, right?

1765
01:13:15,889 --> 01:13:16,921
I'm this.

1766
01:13:17,289 --> 01:13:18,689
Then I'll vote W.

1767
01:13:20,659 --> 01:13:23,761
With all these unexpected variables,

1768
01:13:23,762 --> 01:13:26,031
there's definitely more uncertainty.

1769
01:13:26,032 --> 01:13:28,368
But I'd still rather pair up 1-on-1...

1770
01:13:28,369 --> 01:13:31,202
with someone I trust.

1771
01:13:31,203 --> 01:13:33,904
Then if I get attacked,

1772
01:13:33,905 --> 01:13:35,306
I'll know it could only be my partner.

1773
01:13:35,774 --> 01:13:38,576
(Pairing up 1-on-1)

1774
01:13:38,677 --> 01:13:40,444
(Dark Knight, Superman)

1775
01:13:40,445 --> 01:13:42,446
(Michael, Ted)

1776
01:13:42,447 --> 01:13:44,248
(Nangja, Polar Bear)

1777
01:13:44,249 --> 01:13:46,050
(Go Ae Shin, Benjamin)

1778
01:13:46,051 --> 01:13:47,785
(Sugar, Hippo)

1779
01:13:47,786 --> 01:13:50,087
(Gray, Genie)

1780
01:13:50,389 --> 01:13:51,956
I would like to conduct an ideology check.

1781
01:13:52,324 --> 01:13:55,293
The ideology check will now begin.

1782
01:13:55,994 --> 01:13:57,689
(Ideology check rules)

1783
01:13:57,690 --> 01:13:59,730
(Enter the target's score for each code
and submit your answers.)

1784
01:14:02,901 --> 01:14:06,270
I'm left, so you can tick right.

1785
01:14:06,271 --> 01:14:07,705
Okay.

1786
01:14:08,841 --> 01:14:12,877
We only share one letter of our codes
with each other.

1787
01:14:12,878 --> 01:14:15,413
Then we purposely choose...

1788
01:14:15,414 --> 01:14:19,150
the opposite letter and submit.

1789
01:14:19,151 --> 01:14:21,419
Your ideology check has failed.

1790
01:14:21,954 --> 01:14:24,696
You lose 2,000 dollars
from your personal fund.

1791
01:14:24,697 --> 01:14:25,890
(Genie: 786 dollars, Gray: 0 dollars)

1792
01:14:26,625 --> 01:14:28,325
It keeps me safe.

1793
01:14:28,326 --> 01:14:31,862
So I thought it was a good system.

1794
01:14:32,631 --> 01:14:35,967
(Someone tried to check
your ideology but has failed.)

1795
01:14:36,268 --> 01:14:39,437
We need to hurry
since it takes a while to check.

1796
01:14:39,438 --> 01:14:40,772
It takes time?

1797
01:14:40,773 --> 01:14:42,807
It takes a while.

1798
01:14:42,808 --> 01:14:44,342
I would like to conduct an ideology check.

1799
01:14:44,343 --> 01:14:47,045
The ideology check will now begin.

1800
01:14:48,580 --> 01:14:50,347
- First is right.
- Right?

1801
01:14:50,348 --> 01:14:51,148
Okay.

1802
01:14:51,149 --> 01:14:53,018
(Dark Knight)

1803
01:14:53,019 --> 01:14:55,185
Your ideology check has failed.

1804
01:14:55,186 --> 01:14:57,655
One point for Equality.

1805
01:14:58,891 --> 01:14:59,891
(Michael)

1806
01:14:59,892 --> 01:15:01,159
(Gender)

1807
01:15:01,160 --> 01:15:02,894
The third box is Upper.

1808
01:15:04,429 --> 01:15:05,463
(Class)

1809
01:15:05,464 --> 01:15:07,765
Your ideology check has failed.

1810
01:15:07,766 --> 01:15:10,868
You lose 2,000 dollars
from your personal fund.

1811
01:15:10,869 --> 01:15:12,236
Complete. Failed.

1812
01:15:14,173 --> 01:15:17,075
Who am I? Where am I?
Why am I even doing this?

1813
01:15:20,679 --> 01:15:22,813
Are you all done?

1814
01:15:23,182 --> 01:15:24,649
We also need to choose
the Reporter's question.

1815
01:15:24,650 --> 01:15:25,517
Right. Okay.

1816
01:15:25,518 --> 01:15:28,019
Let's join once Ae Shin is done.

1817
01:15:28,220 --> 01:15:29,887
(Class)

1818
01:15:29,888 --> 01:15:31,122
I have Benjamin.

1819
01:15:32,658 --> 01:15:34,926
Your ideology check has failed.

1820
01:15:34,927 --> 01:15:37,862
You lose 2,000 dollars
from your personal fund.

1821
01:15:37,863 --> 01:15:38,963
(Everyone loses 2,000 dollars.)

1822
01:15:38,964 --> 01:15:39,864
Are we all done?

1823
01:15:40,265 --> 01:15:41,131
Is it over?

1824
01:15:41,900 --> 01:15:44,202
Can I have your attention?

1825
01:15:44,203 --> 01:15:47,771
We were trying to decide
what the Reporter will be asking.

1826
01:15:47,772 --> 01:15:50,107
Let's come up
with three options and discuss.

1827
01:15:50,108 --> 01:15:53,010
What the spy's goal is.

1828
01:15:53,011 --> 01:15:54,678
It'd be good to know that.

1829
01:15:54,679 --> 01:15:56,614
But if we ask that,

1830
01:15:56,615 --> 01:15:58,716
they could say,
"It is to get in your way."

1831
01:15:58,717 --> 01:16:01,018
So let's go with the spy's mission.

1832
01:16:01,019 --> 01:16:02,821
The conditions for the spy to win?

1833
01:16:02,822 --> 01:16:05,222
We need to know that...

1834
01:16:05,223 --> 01:16:07,558
so we can keep the spy in the system.

1835
01:16:08,293 --> 01:16:10,328
We may not be able to get rid of the spy,

1836
01:16:10,329 --> 01:16:13,564
but we can block the risks.

1837
01:16:13,565 --> 01:16:15,733
If there's another purpose,

1838
01:16:15,734 --> 01:16:17,969
we must figure out a way to find the spy.

1839
01:16:17,970 --> 01:16:19,838
Or else,

1840
01:16:19,839 --> 01:16:21,472
our efforts all go to waste.

1841
01:16:22,374 --> 01:16:23,607
But here's what I think.

1842
01:16:23,608 --> 01:16:25,209
Finding the spy...

1843
01:16:25,210 --> 01:16:27,478
or coexisting...

1844
01:16:27,479 --> 01:16:31,615
The Community Center will close soon.

1845
01:16:31,616 --> 01:16:36,554
Today's Reporter must also complete
the information check before the deadline.

1846
01:16:37,122 --> 01:16:38,789
We need to speed up a little.

1847
01:16:38,790 --> 01:16:39,924
We don't have much time.

1848
01:16:39,925 --> 01:16:41,592
Let's just write it all down.

1849
01:16:41,593 --> 01:16:43,094
Then we'll decide on the priorities.

1850
01:16:43,095 --> 01:16:44,395
Write it all down.

1851
01:16:44,630 --> 01:16:45,863
Then the thing...

1852
01:16:45,864 --> 01:16:47,899
They're not likely to tell us
who the spy is.

1853
01:16:47,900 --> 01:16:49,133
That's right.

1854
01:16:49,768 --> 01:16:52,503
There's no pressure.

1855
01:16:52,872 --> 01:16:55,139
It can just go to the bottom.

1856
01:16:55,674 --> 01:16:57,742
- Next?
- How about asking...

1857
01:16:57,743 --> 01:16:59,477
who the spy paired up with?

1858
01:16:59,478 --> 01:17:01,145
We paired up.

1859
01:17:02,281 --> 01:17:05,717
Who did the spy check today?

1860
01:17:05,718 --> 01:17:08,153
- Yes.
- We all were in pairs.

1861
01:17:08,154 --> 01:17:09,354
Excellent.

1862
01:17:09,355 --> 01:17:11,356
Is this the order you want?

1863
01:17:11,357 --> 01:17:13,024
I can't write the last one.
Just remember it.

1864
01:17:13,025 --> 01:17:14,826
If it all fails, just go with,
"Is it male or female?"

1865
01:17:14,827 --> 01:17:15,627
Okay.

1866
01:17:15,628 --> 01:17:17,362
I'll do what I can.

1867
01:17:24,236 --> 01:17:27,438
Today was so productive.

1868
01:17:27,672 --> 01:17:30,975
We made a lot of important decisions.

1869
01:17:30,976 --> 01:17:33,010
We still managed to survive three days.

1870
01:17:48,593 --> 01:17:49,993
I'd like to do an information check.

1871
01:17:50,329 --> 01:17:52,664
Please ask the information
you would like to know.

1872
01:17:55,667 --> 01:17:59,737
(Who is the spy?)

1873
01:18:04,476 --> 01:18:07,812
(Please wait a moment.)

1874
01:18:07,813 --> 01:18:09,646
All three leaders...

1875
01:18:09,647 --> 01:18:11,148
did an incredible job...

1876
01:18:11,149 --> 01:18:12,149
A perfect buildup.

1877
01:18:12,150 --> 01:18:14,085
designing a system for each day.

1878
01:18:14,086 --> 01:18:16,353
True. That was great.

1879
01:18:17,956 --> 01:18:20,291
That information cannot be disclosed.

1880
01:18:26,298 --> 01:18:28,232
Then I'll ask the next question.

1881
01:18:29,101 --> 01:18:32,203
Being the leader keeps getting harder.

1882
01:18:32,871 --> 01:18:34,505
Who did the spy...

1883
01:18:34,906 --> 01:18:38,709
do an ideology check on today?

1884
01:18:38,710 --> 01:18:40,411
That information cannot be disclosed.

1885
01:18:40,412 --> 01:18:42,045
This makes you really think.

1886
01:18:48,320 --> 01:18:50,554
It's a total of nine days, right?
Including the first day.

1887
01:18:50,555 --> 01:18:52,790
Today is Day Three,

1888
01:18:52,791 --> 01:18:54,325
so we made it through one-third.

1889
01:18:54,326 --> 01:18:57,094
And if we survive...

1890
01:18:57,095 --> 01:18:58,396
three more days,

1891
01:18:59,131 --> 01:19:00,798
the attack token is gone.

1892
01:19:07,372 --> 01:19:10,641
(Reporter Information Check)

1893
01:19:34,433 --> 01:19:35,666
Our Reporter is back.

1894
01:19:36,234 --> 01:19:37,267
I think she's back.

1895
01:19:40,238 --> 01:19:41,806
How long did it take?

1896
01:19:41,807 --> 01:19:43,674
How long did I have left?

1897
01:19:43,675 --> 01:19:46,343
I'm so curious why it took so long.

1898
01:19:46,544 --> 01:19:48,011
Well done.

1899
01:19:48,012 --> 01:19:50,681
- What took you so long?
- I took a long time?

1900
01:19:51,583 --> 01:19:53,584
- We'd like a briefing.
- Was it shocking?

1901
01:19:53,585 --> 01:19:55,219
- I'm so curious.
- A Reporter briefing.

1902
01:19:55,954 --> 01:19:57,388
A Reporter briefing?

1903
01:19:57,389 --> 01:19:58,522
Yes, please.

1904
01:19:58,523 --> 01:19:59,356
A Reporter briefing.

1905
01:19:59,357 --> 01:20:00,257
I'm so curious.

1906
01:20:00,258 --> 01:20:02,092
- Drumroll.
- It's the first time, so we can't wait.

1907
01:20:02,093 --> 01:20:03,060
Exactly.

1908
01:20:03,061 --> 01:20:04,862
- She doesn't look so happy.
- True.

1909
01:20:04,863 --> 01:20:05,796
You're right.

1910
01:20:07,633 --> 01:20:10,901
I can't believe I have to go
through all this.

1911
01:20:10,902 --> 01:20:12,636
It's okay.

1912
01:20:13,772 --> 01:20:15,272
I did my best.

1913
01:20:21,313 --> 01:20:22,847
I'm putting up an embargo on this.

1914
01:20:24,049 --> 01:20:25,483
An embargo?

1915
01:20:28,553 --> 01:20:29,820
You're putting up an embargo?

1916
01:20:30,388 --> 01:20:32,489
(Embargo: A media term for a ban
on reporting until a specified time.)

1917
01:20:35,293 --> 01:20:36,627
What's the reason?

1918
01:20:42,000 --> 01:20:43,834
- You can't tell us that either?
- There must be a reason.

1919
01:20:43,835 --> 01:20:45,836
- Why?
- What is it?

1920
01:20:46,304 --> 01:20:48,906
- Why?
- What's that face?

1921
01:20:51,710 --> 01:20:52,643
From the start?

1922
01:20:57,949 --> 01:20:59,983
Are you about to cry?

1923
01:20:59,984 --> 01:21:00,851
She got the information.

1924
01:21:00,852 --> 01:21:02,519
- Why are you tearing up?
- Why are you choked up?

1925
01:21:04,990 --> 01:21:07,258
Did they tell you to get rid of someone?

1926
01:21:07,659 --> 01:21:08,959
I don't think that's it.

1927
01:21:10,228 --> 01:21:11,295
No, it's not that.

1928
01:21:12,330 --> 01:21:14,564
I'm so sad.

1929
01:21:14,565 --> 01:21:17,334
What is it? Why are you crying?

1930
01:21:17,702 --> 01:21:21,172
Exactly. I know, right?

1931
01:21:21,173 --> 01:21:23,306
You put up an embargo...

1932
01:21:23,608 --> 01:21:25,142
because you got an answer.

1933
01:21:25,610 --> 01:21:27,945
But I guess if you reveal it now,

1934
01:21:27,946 --> 01:21:30,480
it will come with a side effect.

1935
01:21:30,481 --> 01:21:33,217
That's what I'll take from this.

1936
01:21:34,119 --> 01:21:35,753
If you didn't get an answer,

1937
01:21:35,954 --> 01:21:39,156
you would've told us
you came out with nothing.

1938
01:21:41,226 --> 01:21:43,527
What was the first question? Identity?

1939
01:21:55,540 --> 01:21:57,074
I'm spent.

1940
01:21:57,075 --> 01:21:58,642
(3 hours ago, money allocation time)

1941
01:22:01,913 --> 01:22:03,981
(Once per day, the spy may view
one resident's full ideology code.)

1942
01:22:03,982 --> 01:22:06,784
(Please choose the resident
you would like to view.)

1943
01:22:06,785 --> 01:22:09,887
Michael.

1944
01:22:11,790 --> 01:22:13,357
I can kill him tomorrow.

1945
01:22:22,634 --> 01:22:23,567
Nangja.

1946
01:22:32,711 --> 01:22:35,546
R, E, W, C.

1947
01:22:37,916 --> 01:22:41,652
Whose code did the spy...

1948
01:22:42,520 --> 01:22:43,386
view today?

1949
01:22:44,456 --> 01:22:47,557
The resident the spy viewed today is...

1950
01:22:48,727 --> 01:22:51,028
Nangja.

1951
01:23:10,048 --> 01:23:11,382
I was dumbfounded.

1952
01:23:11,383 --> 01:23:13,250
Is this why she became the Reporter?

1953
01:23:13,251 --> 01:23:15,919
Those tears were likely fake.

1954
01:23:16,121 --> 01:23:17,921
Why was she crying?
She could've just told us.

1955
01:23:17,922 --> 01:23:19,156
What did she hear?

1956
01:23:19,157 --> 01:23:21,324
I think she was torn about something.

1957
01:23:21,325 --> 01:23:24,528
Whatever it was,
those tears were tears of pain.

1958
01:23:24,529 --> 01:23:27,531
It was strange. Why did she do that?

1959
01:23:27,532 --> 01:23:28,832
What was the meaning of it?

1960
01:23:30,302 --> 01:23:32,770
There is an important announcement.

1961
01:23:32,971 --> 01:23:34,338
What is it now?

1962
01:23:34,739 --> 01:23:38,842
The daily leader system will end
after today, Day Four.

1963
01:23:38,843 --> 01:23:42,245
Starting on Day Six and lasting
until the end of your stay,

1964
01:23:42,246 --> 01:23:46,216
a single leader will take power
for the remainder of your stay.

1965
01:23:46,217 --> 01:23:48,919
- A permanent leader?
- It's like the calm before the storm.

1966
01:23:48,920 --> 01:23:50,087
- A permanent leader.
- Wow.

1967
01:23:50,088 --> 01:23:51,922
So the routine system...

1968
01:23:51,923 --> 01:23:53,090
ends today.

1969
01:23:53,091 --> 01:23:56,359
Special activities will take place
starting tomorrow.

1970
01:23:56,360 --> 01:23:58,229
Routines end today.
